1
00:00:23,756 --> 00:00:27,634
♪ If I didn't care

2
00:00:28,887 --> 00:00:33,140
♪ More than words can say

3
00:00:34,225 --> 00:00:38,395
♪ If I didn't care

4
00:00:39,606 --> 00:00:43,609
♪ Would I feel this way?

5
00:00:44,777 --> 00:00:48,822
♪ If this isn't love

6
00:00:50,575 --> 00:00:54,453
♪ Then why do I thrill?

7
00:00:55,872 --> 00:01:01,627
♪ And what makes my head
go round and round

8
00:01:01,711 --> 00:01:06,506
♪ While my heart stands still?

9
00:01:06,591 --> 00:01:10,219
♪ If I didn't care

10
00:01:11,596 --> 00:01:15,140
♪ Would it be the same?

11
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:22,898
♪ Would my every prayer begin and end

12
00:01:22,982 --> 00:01:25,901
♪ With just your name?

13
00:01:27,570 --> 00:01:30,781
♪ And would I be sure

14
00:01:30,865 --> 00:01:36,745
♪ That this is love beyond compare?

15
00:01:38,414 --> 00:01:40,457
♪ Would all this be true

16
00:01:41,876 --> 00:01:46,964
♪ If I didn't care for you?

17
00:02:04,983 --> 00:02:06,984
Mr. Dufresne,

18
00:02:07,068 --> 00:02:11,321
describe the confrontation you had
with your wife the night she was murdered.

19
00:02:15,952 --> 00:02:18,078
It was very bitter.

20
00:02:18,162 --> 00:02:22,582
She said she was glad I knew,
that she hated all the sneaking around...

21
00:02:24,002 --> 00:02:27,546
And she said she wanted a divorce in Reno.

22
00:02:27,630 --> 00:02:29,464
What was your response?

23
00:02:29,549 --> 00:02:31,508
I told her I would not grant one.

24
00:02:31,592 --> 00:02:34,636
"I'll see you in hell before I see you in Reno."

25
00:02:34,721 --> 00:02:38,390
Those were the words you used, Mr. Dufresne,
according to your neighbors.

26
00:02:39,517 --> 00:02:43,186
If they say so.
I really don't remember. I was upset.

27
00:02:44,314 --> 00:02:47,816
What happened after you argued
with your wife?

28
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:49,818
She packed a bag.

29
00:02:50,903 --> 00:02:54,072
She packed a bag to go and stay with...
Mr. Quentin.

30
00:02:54,157 --> 00:02:56,450
DA: Glenn Quentin.

31
00:02:56,534 --> 00:02:58,618
Golf pro at the Snowdon Hills Country Club.

32
00:02:58,703 --> 00:03:02,581
The man you discovered was your wife's lover.

33
00:03:04,334 --> 00:03:05,709
Did you follow her?

34
00:03:06,961 --> 00:03:09,129
I went to a few bars first.

35
00:03:09,213 --> 00:03:12,090
Later, I drove to his house to confront them.

36
00:03:12,175 --> 00:03:15,761
They weren't home, so I...parked in the turnout,

37
00:03:15,845 --> 00:03:17,429
and waited.

38
00:03:17,513 --> 00:03:19,723
DA: With what intention?

39
00:03:19,807 --> 00:03:21,600
DUFRESNE: I'm not sure.

40
00:03:21,684 --> 00:03:23,894
I was confused.

41
00:03:23,978 --> 00:03:25,645
Drunk.

42
00:03:27,398 --> 00:03:30,400
I think...mostly, I wanted to scare them.

43
00:03:30,485 --> 00:03:33,737
When they arrived,
you went up to the house and murdered them.

44
00:03:33,821 --> 00:03:36,907
No. I was sobering up.

45
00:03:36,991 --> 00:03:39,743
I got back in the car,
and I drove home to sleep it off.

46
00:03:39,827 --> 00:03:43,288
Along the way, I stopped,
and I threw my gun into the Royal River.

47
00:03:43,373 --> 00:03:45,582
I feel I've been very clear on this point.

48
00:03:45,666 --> 00:03:49,461
Well, where I get hazy is where the cleaning
woman shows up the following morning

49
00:03:49,545 --> 00:03:55,217
and finds your wife in bed with her lover,
riddled with .38 caliber bullets.

50
00:03:56,260 --> 00:04:00,514
Now, does that strike you as a fantastic
coincidence, Mr. Dufresne, or is it just me?

51
00:04:00,598 --> 00:04:02,432
Yes, it does.

52
00:04:02,517 --> 00:04:08,605
Yet you still maintain that you threw your gun
into the river before the murders took place?

53
00:04:08,689 --> 00:04:12,484
- That's very convenient.
- It's the truth.

54
00:04:12,568 --> 00:04:17,280
DA: The police dragged that river
for three days, and nary a gun was found.

55
00:04:17,365 --> 00:04:20,158
So, there could be no comparison made
between your gun

56
00:04:20,243 --> 00:04:24,413
and the bullets taken from
the bloodstained corpses of the victims.

57
00:04:25,706 --> 00:04:28,583
And that also...

58
00:04:28,668 --> 00:04:30,669
is very convenient.

59
00:04:30,753 --> 00:04:32,254
Isn’t it, Mr. Dufresne?

60
00:04:33,464 --> 00:04:35,715
Since I am innocent of this crime, sir,

61
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:39,386
I find it decidedly inconvenient
that the gun was never found.

62
00:04:40,555 --> 00:04:42,639
♪ THE INK SPOTS: If I Didn't Care

63
00:04:46,561 --> 00:04:49,813
♪ And would I be sure that this

64
00:04:49,897 --> 00:04:54,526
♪ Is love beyond compare...

65
00:04:58,072 --> 00:05:00,991
DA: Ladies and gentlemen,
you've heard all the evidence.

66
00:05:01,075 --> 00:05:03,660
We have the accused at the scene of the crime.

67
00:05:03,744 --> 00:05:05,579
We have footprints, tire tracks,

68
00:05:05,663 --> 00:05:09,708
bullets strewn on the ground which bear
his fingerprints, a broken bourbon bottle,

69
00:05:09,792 --> 00:05:11,918
likewise with fingerprints.

70
00:05:12,003 --> 00:05:13,670
And most of all,

71
00:05:13,754 --> 00:05:16,047
we have a beautiful young woman and her lover

72
00:05:16,132 --> 00:05:18,216
lying dead in each other's arms.

73
00:05:20,303 --> 00:05:22,554
They had sinned.

74
00:05:24,182 --> 00:05:28,185
But was their crime so great
as to merit a death sentence?

75
00:05:29,353 --> 00:05:31,438
Now, while you think about that...

76
00:05:33,149 --> 00:05:35,650
..think about this.

77
00:05:35,735 --> 00:05:38,278
A revolver holds six bullets.

78
00:05:38,362 --> 00:05:39,738
Not eight.

79
00:05:39,822 --> 00:05:43,283
I submit that this was not a hot-blooded
crime of passion.

80
00:05:43,367 --> 00:05:46,411
That could be understood, if not condoned.

81
00:05:46,496 --> 00:05:49,122
No. This was revenge...

82
00:05:49,207 --> 00:05:53,710
of a much more brutal
and cold-blooded nature. Consider this.

83
00:05:53,794 --> 00:05:56,338
Four bullets per victim.

84
00:05:56,422 --> 00:05:58,798
Not six shots fired, but eight.

85
00:05:58,883 --> 00:06:02,802
That means that he fired the gun empty,

86
00:06:02,887 --> 00:06:05,180
and then stopped to reload...

87
00:06:06,265 --> 00:06:08,850
..so that he could shoot each of them again.

88
00:06:08,935 --> 00:06:11,520
An extra bullet per lover.

89
00:06:11,604 --> 00:06:13,647
Right in the head.

90
00:06:17,693 --> 00:06:22,614
You strike me as a particularly icy
and remorseless man, Mr. Dufresne.

91
00:06:22,698 --> 00:06:24,950
It chills my blood just to look at you.

92
00:06:26,702 --> 00:06:29,663
By the power vested in me
by the state of Maine,

93
00:06:29,747 --> 00:06:33,291
I hereby order you to serve two life sentences,
back to back.

94
00:06:34,377 --> 00:06:37,003
One for each of your victims. So be it.

95
00:07:02,029 --> 00:07:03,697
Sit.

96
00:07:07,743 --> 00:07:10,662
We see by your file
you've served 20 years of a life sentence.

97
00:07:10,746 --> 00:07:12,330
Yes, sir.

98
00:07:12,415 --> 00:07:14,874
You feel you've been rehabilitated?

99
00:07:14,959 --> 00:07:17,168
Oh, yes, sir. Absolutely, sir.

100
00:07:18,796 --> 00:07:20,755
I mean, I learned my lesson.

101
00:07:21,841 --> 00:07:25,093
I can honestly say...I'm a changed man.

102
00:07:28,806 --> 00:07:31,141
I'm no longer a danger to society.

103
00:07:31,225 --> 00:07:33,310
That's God's honest truth.

104
00:08:09,472 --> 00:08:12,265
Hey, Red. How'd it go?

105
00:08:12,350 --> 00:08:14,976
Same old shit. Different day.

106
00:08:15,061 --> 00:08:18,688
Yeah. I know how you feel.
I'm up for rejection next week.

107
00:08:19,857 --> 00:08:22,442
Yeah, I got rejected last week.

108
00:08:22,526 --> 00:08:23,818
It happens.

109
00:08:23,903 --> 00:08:25,487
Hey, Red. Bump me a deck.

110
00:08:25,571 --> 00:08:28,948
Get the fuck out of my face, will you, man?
You've had five packs already.

111
00:08:29,033 --> 00:08:30,241
- Four.
- Five.

112
00:08:31,285 --> 00:08:34,829
RED: There must be a con like me
in every prison in America.

113
00:08:34,914 --> 00:08:36,831
I'm the guy who can get it for you.

114
00:08:36,916 --> 00:08:40,043
Cigarettes, a bag of reefer - if that's your thing -

115
00:08:40,127 --> 00:08:43,713
a bottle of brandy
to celebrate your kid's high-school graduation.

116
00:08:43,798 --> 00:08:46,132
Damn near anything, within reason.

117
00:08:46,217 --> 00:08:48,510
Yes, sir. I'm a regular Sears and Roebuck.

118
00:08:52,848 --> 00:08:56,226
So, when Andy Dufresne came to me in 1949

119
00:08:56,310 --> 00:08:59,646
and asked me to smuggle Rita Hayworth
into the prison for him,

120
00:08:59,730 --> 00:09:01,815
I told him, "No problem."

121
00:09:43,899 --> 00:09:46,192
Andy came to Shawshank Prison

122
00:09:46,277 --> 00:09:50,405
in early 1947, for murdering his wife
and the fella she was banging.

123
00:09:50,489 --> 00:09:54,784
On the outside he had been vice-president
of a large Portland bank.

124
00:09:54,869 --> 00:09:56,870
Good work for a man as young as he was.

125
00:10:02,251 --> 00:10:03,918
Hey, Red.

126
00:10:40,080 --> 00:10:43,625
Do you speak English, butt steak?
You follow this officer.

127
00:10:56,597 --> 00:11:00,975
I haven't seen such a sorry-looking heap
of m-m-maggot shit in all my life.

128
00:11:01,060 --> 00:11:03,144
MAN: Hey, fish! Come over here.

129
00:11:18,202 --> 00:11:21,079
- Come on, fish.

130
00:11:26,544 --> 00:11:28,461
Taking bets today, Red?

131
00:11:28,546 --> 00:11:30,547
Smokes or coins? Better's choice.

132
00:11:30,631 --> 00:11:32,423
Smokes. Put me down for two.

133
00:11:32,508 --> 00:11:34,551
All right. Who's your horse?

134
00:11:34,635 --> 00:11:37,929
That little sack of shit...eighth from the front.

135
00:11:38,013 --> 00:11:39,180
He'll be first.

136
00:11:39,265 --> 00:11:41,474
- Bullshit. I'll take that action.
- Me too.

137
00:11:41,559 --> 00:11:43,810
You're out some smokes, son. Let me tell you.

138
00:11:43,894 --> 00:11:45,812
Heywood, you're so smart, you call it.

139
00:11:45,896 --> 00:11:48,398
I'll take...t-t-the chubby fat-ass there.

140
00:11:48,482 --> 00:11:51,860
The fifth one from the front.
Put me down for a quarter deck.

141
00:11:51,944 --> 00:11:54,821
- Fresh fish!

142
00:11:54,905 --> 00:11:58,992
Fresh fish today! We're reeling them in!

143
00:11:59,076 --> 00:12:03,329
RED: I must admit, I didn't think much of Andy,
first time I laid eyes on him.

144
00:12:03,414 --> 00:12:06,916
Looked like a stiff breeze would blow him over.

145
00:12:07,001 --> 00:12:09,294
That was my first impression of the man.

146
00:12:09,378 --> 00:12:11,087
MAN: What do you say, Red?

147
00:12:11,171 --> 00:12:14,424
That tall drink of water
with the silver spoon up his ass.

148
00:12:14,508 --> 00:12:16,676
That guy? Never happen.

149
00:12:16,760 --> 00:12:17,886
Ten cigarettes.

150
00:12:17,970 --> 00:12:19,137
That's a rich bet.

151
00:12:19,221 --> 00:12:21,431
All right. Who's gonna prove me wrong?

152
00:12:21,515 --> 00:12:23,600
Heywood? Jigger?

153
00:12:23,684 --> 00:12:25,143
Skeet?

154
00:12:25,227 --> 00:12:27,437
Floyd?

155
00:12:27,521 --> 00:12:29,647
Four brave souls.

156
00:12:29,732 --> 00:12:32,567
TANNOY:
Return to your cell blocks for evening count.

157
00:12:32,651 --> 00:12:35,153
All prisoners return to your cell blocks.

158
00:12:56,258 --> 00:12:58,343
Turn to the right. Eyes front.

159
00:13:11,774 --> 00:13:14,776
This is Mr. Hadley. He's Captain of the Guards.

160
00:13:14,860 --> 00:13:17,028
I'm Mr. Norton, the Warden.

161
00:13:17,112 --> 00:13:19,614
You are convicted felons.

162
00:13:19,698 --> 00:13:22,033
That's why they've sent you to me.

163
00:13:22,117 --> 00:13:23,743
Rule number one:

164
00:13:23,827 --> 00:13:25,703
No blasphemy.

165
00:13:25,788 --> 00:13:29,332
I'll not have the Lord's name taken in vain
in my prison.

166
00:13:29,416 --> 00:13:30,792
The other rules...

167
00:13:30,876 --> 00:13:32,585
you'll figure out as you go along.

168
00:13:32,670 --> 00:13:34,462
Any questions?

169
00:13:34,546 --> 00:13:35,755
When do we eat?

170
00:13:44,264 --> 00:13:46,432
HADLEY: You eat when we say you eat!

171
00:13:46,517 --> 00:13:49,894
You shit when we say you shit,
and you piss when we say you piss.

172
00:13:49,979 --> 00:13:52,855
You got that, you maggot-dick motherfucker?

173
00:13:52,940 --> 00:13:54,232
Aarghh!

174
00:13:54,316 --> 00:13:56,275
On your feet.

175
00:13:56,360 --> 00:13:58,152
NORTON: I believe in two things.

176
00:13:58,237 --> 00:14:01,114
Discipline and the Bible.

177
00:14:01,198 --> 00:14:03,032
Here you'll receive both.

178
00:14:04,159 --> 00:14:06,786
Put your trust in the Lord.

179
00:14:06,870 --> 00:14:08,746
Your ass belongs to me.

180
00:14:10,833 --> 00:14:12,917
Welcome to Shawshank.

181
00:14:18,048 --> 00:14:19,465
Unhook 'em.

182
00:14:24,722 --> 00:14:26,597
Turn around.

183
00:14:33,022 --> 00:14:35,356
That's enough.

184
00:14:35,441 --> 00:14:38,192
Move to the end of the cage.

185
00:14:38,277 --> 00:14:39,652
Turn around.

186
00:14:39,737 --> 00:14:40,987
Delouse him.

187
00:14:42,197 --> 00:14:44,073
Turn around.

188
00:14:46,660 --> 00:14:49,829
Move out of the cage, go left.
Pick up your clothes and Bible.

189
00:14:49,913 --> 00:14:51,456
Next man up.

190
00:14:53,584 --> 00:14:56,753
To the right. Right, right, right.

191
00:14:56,837 --> 00:14:58,379
Left, left...

192
00:14:58,464 --> 00:15:02,633
RED: The first night's the toughest.
No doubt about it.

193
00:15:02,718 --> 00:15:05,845
They march you in,
naked as the day you were born,

194
00:15:05,929 --> 00:15:10,016
skin burning and half-blind
from that delousing shit they throw on you.

195
00:15:10,100 --> 00:15:13,061
And when they put you in that cell,

196
00:15:13,145 --> 00:15:15,813
and those bars slam home...

197
00:15:15,898 --> 00:15:18,649
that's when you know it's for real.

198
00:15:18,734 --> 00:15:22,070
Old life blown away in the blink of an eye.

199
00:15:22,154 --> 00:15:23,362
Nothing left,

200
00:15:23,447 --> 00:15:26,157
but all the time in the world to think about it.

201
00:15:27,409 --> 00:15:31,162
Most new fish come close to madness
the first night.

202
00:15:31,246 --> 00:15:34,540
Somebody always breaks down crying.

203
00:15:34,625 --> 00:15:37,085
Happens every time.

204
00:15:37,169 --> 00:15:39,253
The only question is:

205
00:15:39,338 --> 00:15:40,755
Who's it gonna be?

206
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:44,425
It's as good a thing to bet on as any, I guess.

207
00:15:46,345 --> 00:15:48,429
I had my money on Andy Dufresne.

208
00:15:58,065 --> 00:15:59,690
Lights out!

209
00:16:20,212 --> 00:16:23,297
RED: I remember my first night.

210
00:16:23,382 --> 00:16:25,466
Seems like a long time ago.

211
00:16:27,010 --> 00:16:29,470
MAN: Yoo-hoo!

212
00:16:29,555 --> 00:16:32,056
Hey, fish.

213
00:16:32,141 --> 00:16:35,184
Fish, fish, fish, fish, fishy.

214
00:16:35,269 --> 00:16:39,188
- Boy, are you scared of the dark?
- You like it here, new fish?

215
00:16:39,273 --> 00:16:41,607
You'll wish
your daddy never dicked your mommy.

216
00:16:41,692 --> 00:16:45,695
Fishy! Oh, I want me a pork chop. Oh, yes!

217
00:16:45,779 --> 00:16:50,825
You taking this down, new fish?
There's gonna be a quiz later!

218
00:16:50,909 --> 00:16:54,287
- Poke your ass out, give me a first look!
- Shh! Keep it down.

219
00:16:54,371 --> 00:16:57,707
RED: The boys always go fishing
with first-timers.

220
00:16:57,791 --> 00:16:59,834
They don't quit till they reel someone in.

221
00:17:02,504 --> 00:17:05,006
Hey, Fat Ass.

222
00:17:05,090 --> 00:17:06,757
Fat Ass.

223
00:17:07,968 --> 00:17:10,428
Talk to me, boy.

224
00:17:10,512 --> 00:17:13,222
I know you're there. I can hear you breathing.

225
00:17:13,307 --> 00:17:16,225
Don't you listen to these nitwits.
You hear me?

226
00:17:16,310 --> 00:17:18,394
This place ain't so bad.

227
00:17:18,478 --> 00:17:20,104
I tell you what.

228
00:17:21,273 --> 00:17:24,442
I'll introduce you around.
Make you feel right at home.

229
00:17:24,526 --> 00:17:27,111
I know a couple of big old bull queers

230
00:17:27,196 --> 00:17:30,156
that would just love to make your acquaintance.

231
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:33,659
Especially that big, white,
m-mushy butt of yours.

232
00:17:33,744 --> 00:17:35,661
God!

233
00:17:35,746 --> 00:17:37,246
I don't belong here!

234
00:17:37,331 --> 00:17:38,748
MAN: We have a winner!

235
00:17:38,832 --> 00:17:40,583
FAT ASS: I wanna go home!

236
00:17:40,667 --> 00:17:42,501
And it's Fat Ass by a nose!

237
00:17:42,586 --> 00:17:45,755
Hey, it's the fat guy! It's the fat guy!

238
00:17:45,839 --> 00:17:49,675
Fresh fish! Fresh fish! Fresh fish!

239
00:17:49,760 --> 00:17:52,470
FAT ASS: I don't belong here!

240
00:17:52,554 --> 00:17:53,763
ALL: Fresh fish!

241
00:17:53,847 --> 00:17:57,183
I wanna go home. I want my mommy.

242
00:17:57,267 --> 00:18:00,269
MAN: I had your mother. She wasn't that great.

243
00:18:00,354 --> 00:18:01,854
Fresh fish!

244
00:18:03,690 --> 00:18:06,484
What the Christ is this happy horse shit?

245
00:18:06,568 --> 00:18:09,278
He took the Lord's name in vain.
I'm telling the Warden.

246
00:18:09,363 --> 00:18:10,863
With my baton up your ass!

247
00:18:10,948 --> 00:18:12,615
FAT ASS: Let me out of here!

248
00:18:12,699 --> 00:18:16,077
What is your malfunction,
you fat barrel of monkey spunk?

249
00:18:16,161 --> 00:18:17,328
FAT ASS: Please...

250
00:18:17,412 --> 00:18:19,455
I ain't supposed to be here.

251
00:18:19,539 --> 00:18:20,915
Not me!

252
00:18:20,999 --> 00:18:23,459
I ain't gonna count to three, or even one.

253
00:18:23,543 --> 00:18:25,836
Shut the fuck up, or I'll sing you a lullaby!

254
00:18:25,921 --> 00:18:27,755
Shut up, man. Shut up.

255
00:18:27,839 --> 00:18:29,131
There's been a mistake!

256
00:18:29,216 --> 00:18:32,385
You don't understand.
I'm not supposed to be here!

257
00:18:32,469 --> 00:18:33,552
Open that cell.

258
00:18:33,637 --> 00:18:37,807
MAN: Me neither.
You people run this place like a fucking prison!

259
00:18:45,274 --> 00:18:47,316
HADLEY: Son of a bitch.

260
00:18:51,488 --> 00:18:53,572
PRISONER: Take it easy.

261
00:19:16,346 --> 00:19:19,724
If I hear so much as a mouse fart in here
the rest of the night,

262
00:19:19,808 --> 00:19:23,477
I swear by God and sonny Jesus,
you will all visit the infirmary.

263
00:19:23,562 --> 00:19:25,646
Every last motherfucker in here.

264
00:19:29,568 --> 00:19:33,237
Call the trustees.
Take that tub of shit down to the infirmary.

265
00:19:47,544 --> 00:19:52,256
RED: His first night in the joint,
Andy Dufresne cost me two packs of cigarettes.

266
00:19:52,341 --> 00:19:55,217
He never made a sound.

267
00:20:10,734 --> 00:20:13,986
GUARD: Tier 3 North. Clear.

268
00:20:14,071 --> 00:20:16,238
Tier 2 South. Clear.

269
00:20:16,323 --> 00:20:18,199
Tier 3 South. Clear.

270
00:20:18,283 --> 00:20:20,242
Tier 1 North...

271
00:20:20,327 --> 00:20:23,120
- Tier 1 South. Clear.
- Tier 4 South. Clear.

272
00:20:23,205 --> 00:20:25,289
Prepare to roll out.

273
00:20:28,668 --> 00:20:30,753
GUARD: Roll out!

274
00:21:23,640 --> 00:21:26,434
Are... Are you going to eat that?

275
00:21:26,518 --> 00:21:29,437
I hadn't... I hadn't planned on it.

276
00:21:30,689 --> 00:21:31,730
Do you mind?

277
00:21:39,072 --> 00:21:41,699
Ah...that's nice and ripe.

278
00:21:53,295 --> 00:21:55,754
Jake says thank you.

279
00:21:55,839 --> 00:21:58,257
Fell out of his nest over by the plate shop.

280
00:22:00,510 --> 00:22:05,055
I'm going to look after him
until he's big enough to fly.

281
00:22:05,140 --> 00:22:06,932
Oh, no, no. Here he comes.

282
00:22:10,061 --> 00:22:13,981
Morning, fellas. Fine morning, ain't it?

283
00:22:14,065 --> 00:22:16,484
You know why it's a fine morning, don't you?

284
00:22:16,568 --> 00:22:18,986
Come on. Set 'em down.
I want 'em all lined up here,

285
00:22:19,070 --> 00:22:21,864
just like a pretty little chorus line.

286
00:22:21,948 --> 00:22:24,033
Yeah, look at that!

287
00:22:24,117 --> 00:22:26,994
- Look at that. Oh, Lord!
- I can't stand this guy.

288
00:22:28,205 --> 00:22:30,873
Yes. Richmond, Virginia.

289
00:22:30,957 --> 00:22:33,042
- Smell my ass!
- After he smells mine.

290
00:22:33,126 --> 00:22:38,464
Gee, Red, it was a terrible shame
about your horse coming in last, an' all.

291
00:22:38,548 --> 00:22:41,759
But I sure do love that winning horse of mine,
though.

292
00:22:41,843 --> 00:22:44,553
I owe that boy a great big, sloppy kiss
when I see him.

293
00:22:44,638 --> 00:22:47,640
Why don't you give him some of your cigarettes,
instead?

294
00:22:47,724 --> 00:22:49,517
Lucky fuck.

295
00:22:49,601 --> 00:22:51,310
Hey, Tyrell.

296
00:22:51,394 --> 00:22:54,355
You pull infirmary duty this week?

297
00:22:54,439 --> 00:22:56,148
How's that horse of mine doing?

298
00:22:56,233 --> 00:22:57,691
Dead.

299
00:22:57,776 --> 00:23:03,155
Hadley busted his head up pretty good.
The doc had already gone home for the night.

300
00:23:03,156 --> 00:23:05,908
Poor bastard lay there till this morning.

301
00:23:05,992 --> 00:23:08,827
By then, hell, there weren't nothing we could do.

302
00:23:14,626 --> 00:23:16,210
What was his name?

303
00:23:19,464 --> 00:23:20,839
What did you say?

304
00:23:22,717 --> 00:23:25,094
I was just wondering if anyone knew his name.

305
00:23:25,178 --> 00:23:27,888
What the fuck do you care, new fish?

306
00:23:29,349 --> 00:23:32,726
It doesn't fuckin' matter what his name was.
He's dead.

307
00:23:52,080 --> 00:23:54,415
Hey...

308
00:23:54,499 --> 00:23:57,251
Anybody come at you yet?

309
00:23:58,753 --> 00:24:00,754
Anybody get to you yet?

310
00:24:01,881 --> 00:24:04,925
Hey, we all need friends in here.

311
00:24:05,010 --> 00:24:06,927
I could be a friend to you.

312
00:24:09,389 --> 00:24:11,056
Hey...

313
00:24:15,895 --> 00:24:17,771
Hard to get.

314
00:24:19,441 --> 00:24:21,108
I like that.

315
00:24:27,741 --> 00:24:31,035
RED: Andy kept pretty much to himself at first.

316
00:24:31,119 --> 00:24:33,579
I guess he had a lot on his mind,

317
00:24:33,663 --> 00:24:35,873
trying to adapt to life on the inside.

318
00:24:37,417 --> 00:24:40,127
It wasn't until a month went by
that he opened his mouth

319
00:24:40,211 --> 00:24:42,504
to say more than two words to somebody.

320
00:24:44,424 --> 00:24:46,467
As it turned out...

321
00:24:46,551 --> 00:24:48,469
that somebody was me.

322
00:24:52,057 --> 00:24:56,226
TANNOY: Officers Russell and Burwell
report to the duty officer.

323
00:24:57,270 --> 00:25:00,022
I'm Andy Dufresne.

324
00:25:00,106 --> 00:25:02,191
The wife-killing banker.

325
00:25:04,027 --> 00:25:05,736
Why did you do it?

326
00:25:05,820 --> 00:25:07,446
I didn't, since you ask.

327
00:25:07,530 --> 00:25:10,574
You're gonna fit right in!

328
00:25:10,659 --> 00:25:13,827
Everybody in here's innocent.
Didn't you know that?

329
00:25:13,912 --> 00:25:17,247
- Heywood, what are you in here for?
- Didn't do it. Lawyer fucked me.

330
00:25:20,168 --> 00:25:22,961
Rumor has it you're a real cold fish.

331
00:25:23,046 --> 00:25:26,799
You think your shit smells sweeter than most.
Is that right?

332
00:25:26,883 --> 00:25:29,218
- What do you think?
- To tell you the truth,

333
00:25:29,302 --> 00:25:32,388
I haven't made up my mind. (Chuckles)

334
00:25:32,472 --> 00:25:36,558
I understand
you're a man that knows how to get things.

335
00:25:36,643 --> 00:25:40,145
I'm known to locate certain things,
from time to time.

336
00:25:40,230 --> 00:25:43,232
- I wonder if you might get me a rock hammer.
- A what?

337
00:25:43,316 --> 00:25:44,692
A rock hammer.

338
00:25:44,776 --> 00:25:47,069
- What is it, and why?
- What do you care?

339
00:25:48,947 --> 00:25:51,824
Well, if it was a toothbrush, I'd just quote a price,

340
00:25:51,908 --> 00:25:54,493
but then a toothbrush is a non-lethal object.

341
00:25:55,912 --> 00:25:57,621
Fair enough.

342
00:25:57,706 --> 00:26:00,165
A rock hammer
is about six or seven inches long.

343
00:26:00,250 --> 00:26:01,875
Looks like a miniature pickaxe.

344
00:26:01,960 --> 00:26:03,085
Pickaxe?

345
00:26:03,169 --> 00:26:05,254
- For rocks.
- Rocks.

346
00:26:06,923 --> 00:26:09,091
Quartz?

347
00:26:09,175 --> 00:26:10,843
Quartz.

348
00:26:12,095 --> 00:26:14,471
And some mica.

349
00:26:14,556 --> 00:26:15,723
Shale.

350
00:26:16,808 --> 00:26:18,392
Limestone.

351
00:26:18,476 --> 00:26:20,394
So?

352
00:26:20,478 --> 00:26:22,563
So, I'm a rock hound.

353
00:26:22,647 --> 00:26:25,858
At least, I was in my old life.
I'd like to be again on a limited basis.

354
00:26:25,942 --> 00:26:28,068
Or maybe you want
to hammer somebody's skull.

355
00:26:28,153 --> 00:26:29,611
No, sir.

356
00:26:30,739 --> 00:26:32,322
I have no enemies here.

357
00:26:32,407 --> 00:26:34,783
No? Wait a while.

358
00:26:36,327 --> 00:26:38,495
Word gets around.

359
00:26:38,580 --> 00:26:41,707
The Sisters have taken quite a liking to you.

360
00:26:41,791 --> 00:26:43,584
Especially Bogs.

361
00:26:44,669 --> 00:26:48,881
I don't suppose it would help any
if I explained to them I'm not homosexual.

362
00:26:48,965 --> 00:26:50,466
RED: Neither are they.

363
00:26:50,550 --> 00:26:53,260
You have to be human first. They don't qualify.

364
00:26:58,516 --> 00:27:03,061
Bull queers take by force.
It's all they want or understand.

365
00:27:03,146 --> 00:27:06,774
If I were you,
I'd grow eyes in the back of my head.

366
00:27:06,858 --> 00:27:08,192
Thanks for the advice.

367
00:27:08,276 --> 00:27:09,860
Well, that's free.

368
00:27:09,944 --> 00:27:12,196
You understand my concern.

369
00:27:12,280 --> 00:27:14,823
Well, if there's any trouble,
I won't use the hammer.

370
00:27:14,908 --> 00:27:19,119
Then I guess you want to escape.
Tunnel under the wall, maybe.

371
00:27:20,580 --> 00:27:22,372
Did I miss something? What's funny?

372
00:27:22,457 --> 00:27:25,501
You'll understand
when you see the rock hammer.

373
00:27:27,378 --> 00:27:29,379
What's an item like this usually go for?

374
00:27:29,464 --> 00:27:32,508
Seven dollars in any rock and gem shop.

375
00:27:32,592 --> 00:27:35,302
My normal mark-up's 20 per cent.

376
00:27:35,303 --> 00:27:37,846
But this is a specialty item.

377
00:27:37,931 --> 00:27:40,516
The risk goes up, the price goes up.

378
00:27:40,517 --> 00:27:42,476
Let's make it an even ten bucks.

379
00:27:42,560 --> 00:27:44,436
Ten it is.

380
00:27:45,522 --> 00:27:47,940
Waste of money, if you ask me.

381
00:27:48,024 --> 00:27:49,566
Why's that?

382
00:27:50,652 --> 00:27:53,695
Folks around this joint love surprise inspections.

383
00:27:55,281 --> 00:27:57,574
If they find it, you're gonna lose it.

384
00:27:57,659 --> 00:27:59,785
If they do catch you with it, you don't know me.

385
00:27:59,869 --> 00:28:02,079
Mention my name, we never do business again.

386
00:28:02,163 --> 00:28:05,290
Not for shoelaces or a stick of gum.
Now, you got that?

387
00:28:06,793 --> 00:28:08,210
I understand.

388
00:28:08,294 --> 00:28:09,711
Thank you...

389
00:28:09,712 --> 00:28:11,338
Mr. er...?

390
00:28:11,422 --> 00:28:13,882
Red. The name's Red.

391
00:28:13,883 --> 00:28:14,925
Red?

392
00:28:15,009 --> 00:28:17,761
Why do they call you that?

393
00:28:21,432 --> 00:28:23,517
Maybe it's because I'm Irish.

394
00:28:31,484 --> 00:28:34,528
RED: I could see
why some of the boys took him for snobby.

395
00:28:34,612 --> 00:28:38,031
He had a quiet way about him.

396
00:28:38,116 --> 00:28:42,578
A walk and a talk
that just wasn't normal around here.

397
00:28:42,662 --> 00:28:44,204
He strolled...

398
00:28:44,289 --> 00:28:48,625
like a man in a park,
without a care or worry in the world.

399
00:28:48,710 --> 00:28:53,422
Like he had on an invisible coat
that would shield him from this place.

400
00:28:53,506 --> 00:28:57,676
Yeah. I think it would be fair to say...

401
00:28:57,677 --> 00:28:59,761
I liked Andy from the start.

402
00:29:01,514 --> 00:29:04,725
Let's go!
Some of us have got a schedule to keep.

403
00:29:08,021 --> 00:29:10,105
Move it! Come on. Move it!

404
00:29:13,735 --> 00:29:16,862
Bob, how are you doing?
How's the wife treating you?

405
00:29:26,581 --> 00:29:28,040
Easy.

406
00:29:28,124 --> 00:29:30,208
Keep it moving.

407
00:29:33,004 --> 00:29:34,254
Red...

408
00:29:54,776 --> 00:29:58,987
RED: Andy was right. I finally got the joke.

409
00:29:59,072 --> 00:30:03,575
It would take a man about 600 years
to tunnel under the wall with one of these.

410
00:30:07,914 --> 00:30:09,039
Book?

411
00:30:09,123 --> 00:30:10,999
Not today.

412
00:30:16,339 --> 00:30:18,423
- Book?
- No.

413
00:30:20,218 --> 00:30:21,426
Hey, Brooksy.

414
00:30:24,722 --> 00:30:26,807
Delivery for Dufresne.

415
00:30:33,898 --> 00:30:39,528
Book?

416
00:30:42,532 --> 00:30:44,658
Dufresne...

417
00:30:44,742 --> 00:30:46,910
Here's your book.

418
00:30:51,332 --> 00:30:53,000
Thanks.

419
00:30:56,838 --> 00:31:00,048
Dufresne! We're running low on Hexlite.

420
00:31:00,133 --> 00:31:02,217
Get on back and fetch us up some.

421
00:31:39,422 --> 00:31:41,381
If you get this in your eyes, it blinds you.

422
00:31:41,466 --> 00:31:44,217
Honey...hush.

423
00:31:56,272 --> 00:31:58,690
That's it. You fight.

424
00:31:58,775 --> 00:32:00,358
It's better that way.

425
00:32:08,785 --> 00:32:11,620
RED: I wish I could tell you
that Andy fought the good fight,

426
00:32:11,704 --> 00:32:14,247
and the Sisters let him be.

427
00:32:14,332 --> 00:32:16,333
I wish I could tell you that.

428
00:32:16,417 --> 00:32:18,502
But prison is no fairy-tale world.

429
00:32:20,463 --> 00:32:22,589
He never said who did it.

430
00:32:22,673 --> 00:32:25,717
But we all knew.

431
00:32:29,055 --> 00:32:31,932
Things went on like that for a while.

432
00:32:32,016 --> 00:32:34,643
Prison life consists of routine...

433
00:32:34,727 --> 00:32:36,937
and then more routine.

434
00:32:39,315 --> 00:32:43,151
Every so often,
Andy would show up with fresh bruises.

435
00:32:44,320 --> 00:32:46,363
The Sisters kept at him.

436
00:32:46,447 --> 00:32:50,575
Sometimes he was able to fight 'em off.
Sometimes not.

437
00:32:50,660 --> 00:32:53,912
And that's how it went for Andy.

438
00:32:53,996 --> 00:32:56,540
That was his routine.

439
00:32:56,624 --> 00:33:00,418
I do believe
those first two years were the worst for him.

440
00:33:00,503 --> 00:33:02,754
And I also believe,

441
00:33:02,839 --> 00:33:07,384
if things had gone on that way,
this place would have got the best of him.

442
00:33:07,468 --> 00:33:10,428
But then, in the spring of 1949,

443
00:33:10,513 --> 00:33:12,514
the powers that be decided that...

444
00:33:12,598 --> 00:33:16,017
The roof of the license-plate factory
needs resurfacing.

445
00:33:16,102 --> 00:33:18,854
I need a dozen volunteers for a week's work.

446
00:33:20,022 --> 00:33:22,149
As you know,

447
00:33:22,233 --> 00:33:25,944
special detail carries with it special privileges.

448
00:33:26,028 --> 00:33:28,113
RED: It was outdoor detail...

449
00:33:29,615 --> 00:33:33,201
..and May is one damn fine month
to be working outdoors.

450
00:33:33,286 --> 00:33:34,911
Stay in line there.

451
00:33:34,996 --> 00:33:37,914
More than a hundred men
volunteered for the job.

452
00:33:42,295 --> 00:33:44,588
Wallace E Unger.

453
00:33:45,756 --> 00:33:48,216
Ellis Redding.

454
00:33:48,301 --> 00:33:51,136
Wouldn't you know it?

455
00:33:51,220 --> 00:33:53,805
Me and some fellows I know
were among the names.

456
00:33:53,890 --> 00:33:55,974
GUARD: Andrew Dufresne.

457
00:33:56,058 --> 00:33:59,603
RED: It only cost us a pack of smokes per man.

458
00:33:59,687 --> 00:34:02,397
I made my usual 20 per cent, of course.

459
00:34:02,481 --> 00:34:06,067
HADLEY: So, this big-shot lawyer
calls me long-distance from Texas.

460
00:34:06,152 --> 00:34:07,861
I say, "Yeah?"

461
00:34:07,945 --> 00:34:11,656
He says, "Sorry to inform you,
but your brother just died."

462
00:34:11,741 --> 00:34:14,242
Oh, damn, Byron. I'm sorry to hear that.

463
00:34:14,327 --> 00:34:15,702
I'm not. He was an asshole.

464
00:34:15,786 --> 00:34:18,121
Ran off years ago.
Figured him for dead, anyway.

465
00:34:18,206 --> 00:34:22,167
So, anyway, this lawyer fella says to me,
"Your brother died a rich man."

466
00:34:22,251 --> 00:34:24,961
Oil wells and shit. Close to a million bucks.

467
00:34:25,046 --> 00:34:27,505
GUARD: A million bucks?

468
00:34:27,590 --> 00:34:29,966
Fucking incredible
how lucky some assholes get.

469
00:34:30,051 --> 00:34:32,594
Jeez-Louise! Are you gonna see any of that?

470
00:34:32,678 --> 00:34:35,764
35,000. That's what he left me.

471
00:34:35,848 --> 00:34:37,766
- Dollars?
- Yeah.

472
00:34:37,850 --> 00:34:40,977
Holy shit! That's great.
That's like winning the sweepstakes.

473
00:34:41,062 --> 00:34:42,270
Isn’t it?

474
00:34:42,355 --> 00:34:45,398
Dumb shit. What do you think
the government's gonna do to me?

475
00:34:45,483 --> 00:34:48,026
Take a big, wet bite out of my ass, is what.

476
00:34:48,027 --> 00:34:52,197
Poor Byron! Terrible fucking luck, huh?

477
00:34:52,281 --> 00:34:54,950
RED: Crying shame.

478
00:34:55,034 --> 00:34:56,701
Some people really got it awful!

479
00:34:56,786 --> 00:34:58,328
Andy, are you nuts?

480
00:34:58,412 --> 00:35:00,538
Keep your eyes on your mop, man. Andy!

481
00:35:00,539 --> 00:35:03,583
GUARD: Well, you'll pay some tax,
but you'll still end up...

482
00:35:03,668 --> 00:35:06,503
Oh, yeah, yeah.
Maybe enough to buy a new car. Then what?

483
00:35:06,587 --> 00:35:08,964
I've got to pay tax on the car.

484
00:35:09,048 --> 00:35:12,926
Repair, maintenance, goddamn kids
pestering me to take them for a ride...

485
00:35:13,010 --> 00:35:16,930
And at the end of the year, you figure the tax
wrong, you pay them out of your own pocket.

486
00:35:17,014 --> 00:35:19,182
I tell you...Uncle Sam!

487
00:35:19,267 --> 00:35:23,019
He puts his hand in your shirt
and squeezes your tit till it's purple.

488
00:35:23,104 --> 00:35:25,814
- Andy! Andy!
- What's he doing?

489
00:35:25,898 --> 00:35:28,233
- Getting himself killed.
- Keep tarring.

490
00:35:28,317 --> 00:35:31,403
Some brother! Shit. (Spits)

491
00:35:31,487 --> 00:35:32,529
Hey!

492
00:35:35,866 --> 00:35:38,076
Mr. Hadley...

493
00:35:38,077 --> 00:35:39,536
do you trust your wife?

494
00:35:41,080 --> 00:35:42,831
Oh, that's funny.

495
00:35:42,915 --> 00:35:45,792
You're gonna look funnier
sucking my dick with no teeth.

496
00:35:45,876 --> 00:35:49,337
I mean, do you think
she'd go behind your back, try to hamstring you?

497
00:35:50,423 --> 00:35:52,299
That's it. Step aside, Mert.

498
00:35:52,383 --> 00:35:53,883
This fucker's having an accident.

499
00:35:53,968 --> 00:35:55,135
He's gonna push him off.

500
00:35:55,219 --> 00:35:59,347
Cos if you do trust her,
there's no reason you can't keep that 35,000.

501
00:35:59,432 --> 00:36:01,308
- What did you say?
- 35,000.

502
00:36:01,392 --> 00:36:03,101
- 35,000?
- All of it.

503
00:36:03,102 --> 00:36:05,437
- All of it?
- Every penny.

504
00:36:05,521 --> 00:36:07,355
You'd better start making sense.

505
00:36:07,440 --> 00:36:09,774
If you want to keep the money,
give it to your wife.

506
00:36:09,859 --> 00:36:13,987
The IRS allows a one-time-only gift
to your spouse for up to $60,000.

507
00:36:14,071 --> 00:36:15,613
Bullshit! Tax-free?

508
00:36:15,614 --> 00:36:18,783
Tax-free. The IRS can't touch one cent.

509
00:36:18,868 --> 00:36:21,453
You're the smart banker what killed his wife.

510
00:36:21,537 --> 00:36:25,081
Why should I believe a smart banker like you?
So I can end up in here with you?

511
00:36:25,166 --> 00:36:26,666
It's legal. Ask the IRS.

512
00:36:26,751 --> 00:36:29,961
They'll say the same thing.
But I feel stupid telling you this.

513
00:36:30,046 --> 00:36:32,339
I'm sure you would have investigated the matter.

514
00:36:32,423 --> 00:36:36,509
Yeah, I don't need no banker to tell me
where the bears shit in the buckwheat.

515
00:36:36,594 --> 00:36:39,637
Of course not, but you do need someone
to set up the tax-free gift.

516
00:36:39,722 --> 00:36:41,598
It'll cost you. A lawyer.

517
00:36:41,682 --> 00:36:44,017
A bunch of ball-washing bastards.

518
00:36:44,101 --> 00:36:47,937
I suppose I could set it up for you.
That would save you some money.

519
00:36:47,938 --> 00:36:50,315
If you get the forms, I'll prepare them for you.

520
00:36:50,399 --> 00:36:52,275
Nearly free of charge.

521
00:36:53,361 --> 00:36:56,738
I'd only ask three beers apiece
for each of my co-workers.

522
00:36:56,822 --> 00:36:59,240
GUARD:
Co-workers! Get him! That's rich, ain't it?

523
00:36:59,325 --> 00:37:02,786
I think a man working outdoors
feels more like a man

524
00:37:02,870 --> 00:37:04,621
if he can have a bottle of suds.

525
00:37:04,705 --> 00:37:07,749
That's only my opinion...sir.

526
00:37:11,170 --> 00:37:12,712
What are you jimmies staring at?

527
00:37:12,797 --> 00:37:13,797
Back to work!

528
00:37:13,881 --> 00:37:15,840
GUARD: Let's go. Work.

529
00:37:24,225 --> 00:37:29,729
RED: And that's how it came to pass...
that on the second-to-last day of the job,

530
00:37:29,814 --> 00:37:34,484
the convict crew that tarred the plate-factory roof
in the spring of '49...

531
00:37:34,568 --> 00:37:37,779
wound up sitting in a row
at ten o'clock in the morning,

532
00:37:37,863 --> 00:37:41,741
drinking icy-cold, Bohemia-style beer...

533
00:37:41,826 --> 00:37:47,038
courtesy of the hardest screw that ever
walked a turn at Shawshank State Prison.

534
00:37:47,123 --> 00:37:49,207
Drink up while it's cold, ladies.

535
00:37:50,584 --> 00:37:54,754
The colossal prick
even managed to sound magnanimous.

536
00:37:56,507 --> 00:38:01,136
We sat and drank with the sun on our shoulders,
and felt like free men.

537
00:38:01,220 --> 00:38:04,722
Hell, we could have been tarring the roof
of one of our own houses.

538
00:38:04,807 --> 00:38:08,101
We were the lords of all creation.

539
00:38:08,185 --> 00:38:12,730
As for Andy,
he spent that break hunkered in the shade...

540
00:38:12,815 --> 00:38:15,525
a strange little smile on his face,

541
00:38:15,609 --> 00:38:17,694
watching us drink his beer.

542
00:38:24,452 --> 00:38:25,994
Hey...

543
00:38:26,078 --> 00:38:27,370
Want a cold one, Andy?

544
00:38:27,455 --> 00:38:29,122
No, thanks.

545
00:38:29,206 --> 00:38:30,999
I gave up drinking.

546
00:38:34,837 --> 00:38:38,006
RED: You could argue
he'd done it to curry favor with the guards.

547
00:38:38,090 --> 00:38:40,925
Or maybe make a few friends among us cons.

548
00:38:43,179 --> 00:38:46,639
Me, I think he did it just to feel normal again.

549
00:38:47,725 --> 00:38:49,809
If only for a short while.

550
00:38:52,688 --> 00:38:55,023
RED: King me.

551
00:38:55,107 --> 00:38:57,525
Chess. Now, there's a game of kings.

552
00:38:57,610 --> 00:39:00,153
- What?
- Civilized, strategic.

553
00:39:00,237 --> 00:39:02,572
And a total fucking mystery.

554
00:39:02,656 --> 00:39:03,865
I hate it.

555
00:39:03,949 --> 00:39:05,867
Maybe you'll let me teach you someday.

556
00:39:05,951 --> 00:39:08,411
Yeah. Sure.

557
00:39:08,496 --> 00:39:10,622
I thought of getting a board together.

558
00:39:10,706 --> 00:39:13,500
Well, I'm the right man.
I'm the guy that can get things.

559
00:39:13,584 --> 00:39:15,084
We might do business on a board.

560
00:39:15,169 --> 00:39:17,962
But I want to carve the pieces myself.

561
00:39:18,047 --> 00:39:21,633
One side in alabaster, the opposing side
in soapstone. What do you think?

562
00:39:21,717 --> 00:39:23,635
I think it'll take years.

563
00:39:23,719 --> 00:39:26,304
Well, years I got.
What I don't have are the rocks.

564
00:39:26,388 --> 00:39:28,556
Pickings are pretty slim in the yard.

565
00:39:28,641 --> 00:39:30,600
Pebbles, mostly.

566
00:39:34,939 --> 00:39:37,815
Andy, we're getting to be kinda friends,
aren't we?

567
00:39:37,900 --> 00:39:39,567
Yeah, I guess.

568
00:39:40,694 --> 00:39:43,112
Can I ask you something?

569
00:39:43,197 --> 00:39:45,198
Why did you do it?

570
00:39:46,408 --> 00:39:50,286
I'm innocent, Red. Just like everybody else here.

571
00:39:54,583 --> 00:39:56,668
What are you in for?

572
00:39:58,671 --> 00:39:59,671
Murder.

573
00:39:59,755 --> 00:40:01,172
Same as you.

574
00:40:01,257 --> 00:40:02,423
Innocent?

575
00:40:05,094 --> 00:40:07,178
Only guilty man in Shawshank.

576
00:41:06,655 --> 00:41:08,239
Where's the canary?

577
00:41:08,324 --> 00:41:09,657
How did you know?

578
00:41:09,658 --> 00:41:10,825
How did I know what?

579
00:41:10,909 --> 00:41:13,328
So, you don't know? Come.

580
00:41:16,081 --> 00:41:18,207
This is where the canary is, Johnny.

581
00:41:20,586 --> 00:41:24,589
Quite a surprise to hear a woman singing
in my house, eh, Johnny?

582
00:41:27,509 --> 00:41:29,886
That's quite a...

583
00:41:29,970 --> 00:41:31,638
surprise.

584
00:41:34,141 --> 00:41:35,850
Red...

585
00:41:35,934 --> 00:41:37,727
Wait, wait, wait, wait.

586
00:41:37,811 --> 00:41:39,646
Here she comes.

587
00:41:39,730 --> 00:41:42,607
This is the part I like -
when she does that shit with her hair.

588
00:41:42,691 --> 00:41:45,610
Yeah, I know. I've seen it three times this month.

589
00:41:45,694 --> 00:41:47,695
Gilda, are you decent?

590
00:41:47,780 --> 00:41:49,822
Me?

591
00:41:51,533 --> 00:41:53,576
Hah! God, I love it!

592
00:41:59,458 --> 00:42:03,252
I understand
you're a man that knows how to get things.

593
00:42:03,337 --> 00:42:07,215
Yeah, I'm known to locate certain things
from time to time. What do you want?

594
00:42:07,299 --> 00:42:09,801
- Rita Hayworth.
- What?

595
00:42:09,885 --> 00:42:11,719
Can you get her?

596
00:42:12,805 --> 00:42:16,265
So, this is Johnny Farrell.
I've heard a lot about you, Johnny.

597
00:42:16,350 --> 00:42:18,226
Take a few weeks.

598
00:42:18,310 --> 00:42:19,519
Weeks?

599
00:42:19,520 --> 00:42:24,774
Well, yeah, Andy. I don't have her stuffed down
the front of my pants right now, I'm sorry to say.

600
00:42:24,858 --> 00:42:27,360
But I'll get her. Relax.

601
00:42:29,613 --> 00:42:31,114
Thanks.

602
00:42:31,198 --> 00:42:33,199
- It was a surprise, Mr. Farrell.
- It was.

603
00:42:33,283 --> 00:42:34,325
MEN: Sit down.

604
00:42:34,410 --> 00:42:36,327
Did you tell him what I'm doing here?

605
00:42:36,412 --> 00:42:39,247
No, I wanted to save that as a surprise, too.

606
00:42:39,331 --> 00:42:40,998
Hang on to your hat, Mr. Farrell...

607
00:42:51,093 --> 00:42:53,344
- Take a walk.
- I gotta change the reel.

608
00:42:53,429 --> 00:42:55,513
I said fuck off!

609
00:43:00,352 --> 00:43:03,146
Ain't you gonna scream?

610
00:43:04,523 --> 00:43:06,315
Let's get this over with.

611
00:43:10,446 --> 00:43:12,071
He broke my fuckin' nose!

612
00:43:22,583 --> 00:43:26,627
Now...I'm gonna open my fly...

613
00:43:26,712 --> 00:43:29,672
and you're gonna swallow
what I give you to swallow.

614
00:43:30,758 --> 00:43:32,550
Then you're gonna swallow Rooster's.

615
00:43:32,634 --> 00:43:35,470
You broke his nose,
he ought to have something to show for it.

616
00:43:35,554 --> 00:43:37,764
Anything you put in my mouth,
you're gonna lose.

617
00:43:37,848 --> 00:43:40,600
No. You don't understand.

618
00:43:40,684 --> 00:43:43,978
You do that, and I'll put
all eight inches of this steel in your ear.

619
00:43:43,979 --> 00:43:45,062
All right.

620
00:43:45,147 --> 00:43:49,776
But you should know that sudden, serious
brain injury causes the victim to bite down hard.

621
00:43:51,028 --> 00:43:53,446
In fact, I hear the bite reflex is so strong,

622
00:43:53,530 --> 00:43:58,326
they have to pry the victim's jaws open...
with a crowbar.

623
00:44:01,163 --> 00:44:02,789
Where did you get this shit?

624
00:44:02,873 --> 00:44:04,540
I read it.

625
00:44:05,626 --> 00:44:08,503
Know how to read, you ignorant fuck?

626
00:44:10,005 --> 00:44:11,881
Honey...

627
00:44:13,133 --> 00:44:15,510
..you shouldn't.

628
00:44:17,554 --> 00:44:20,556
RED: Bogs didn't put anything in Andy's mouth...

629
00:44:20,641 --> 00:44:23,017
and neither did his friends.

630
00:44:23,101 --> 00:44:26,395
What they did do...
is beat him within an inch of his life.

631
00:44:27,606 --> 00:44:29,607
Andy spent a month in the infirmary.

632
00:44:30,651 --> 00:44:33,152
Bogs spent a week in the hole.

633
00:44:37,616 --> 00:44:39,242
Time's up, Bogs.

634
00:44:42,913 --> 00:44:44,997
It's your world, boss.

635
00:44:47,084 --> 00:44:50,503
TANNOY:
Return to your cell blocks for evening count.

636
00:44:50,587 --> 00:44:52,755
All prisoners report for lockdown.

637
00:45:08,313 --> 00:45:09,605
What?

638
00:45:22,077 --> 00:45:24,787
GUARD: - Where's he going?
HADLEY: - Grab his ankles.

639
00:45:26,373 --> 00:45:28,875
No! No!

640
00:45:28,959 --> 00:45:30,334
No!

641
00:45:30,419 --> 00:45:31,752
Help me...

642
00:45:31,837 --> 00:45:33,129
Aaarghh!

643
00:45:35,424 --> 00:45:38,092
RED:
Two things never happened again after that.

644
00:45:38,176 --> 00:45:41,012
The Sisters never laid a finger on Andy again...

645
00:45:42,222 --> 00:45:44,932
..and Bogs never walked again.

646
00:45:46,059 --> 00:45:49,478
They transferred him
to a minimum-security hospital upstate.

647
00:45:49,563 --> 00:45:55,276
To my knowledge, he lived out the rest
of his days drinking his food through a straw.

648
00:45:55,360 --> 00:46:00,031
I'm thinking Andy could use a nice welcome
back when he gets out of the infirmary.

649
00:46:01,158 --> 00:46:04,952
Sounds good to us.
I figure we owe him that much for the beer.

650
00:46:06,330 --> 00:46:08,998
The man likes to play chess.

651
00:46:09,082 --> 00:46:11,167
Let's get him some rocks.

652
00:46:43,575 --> 00:46:45,451
Guys...

653
00:46:45,535 --> 00:46:47,912
I got one. I got one. Look.

654
00:46:50,332 --> 00:46:52,416
Heywood, that isn't soapstone.

655
00:46:52,501 --> 00:46:54,710
And it ain't alabaster, either.

656
00:46:54,795 --> 00:46:56,420
What are you, a fucking geologist?

657
00:46:56,505 --> 00:46:58,547
He's right. It ain't.

658
00:46:58,632 --> 00:46:59,882
Well, what the hell is it?

659
00:46:59,967 --> 00:47:02,510
RED: A horse apple.

660
00:47:02,594 --> 00:47:05,721
- Bullshit.
- No, horse shit. Petrified.

661
00:47:12,104 --> 00:47:14,981
- Oh, Jesus Christ!

662
00:47:15,065 --> 00:47:17,024
Oh, damn!

663
00:47:17,109 --> 00:47:21,070
RED: Despite a few hitches,
the boys came through in fine style.

664
00:47:22,280 --> 00:47:24,907
And by the weekend he was due back,
we had enough rocks

665
00:47:24,992 --> 00:47:28,035
to keep him busy till rapture.

666
00:47:28,120 --> 00:47:30,830
I also got a big shipment in that week.

667
00:47:30,914 --> 00:47:33,457
Cigarettes. Chewing gum.

668
00:47:33,542 --> 00:47:35,042
Sipping whisky.

669
00:47:35,127 --> 00:47:38,671
Playing cards with naked ladies on them.
You name it.

670
00:47:38,755 --> 00:47:41,716
And of course, the most important item.

671
00:47:43,468 --> 00:47:45,553
Rita Hayworth herself.

672
00:48:19,713 --> 00:48:22,548
GUARD: OK, look alive! Open all tiers.

673
00:48:22,632 --> 00:48:24,967
Heads up. They're tossing the cells.

674
00:48:25,052 --> 00:48:27,303
Heads up. They're tossing the cells.

675
00:48:29,431 --> 00:48:31,307
119.

676
00:48:32,809 --> 00:48:34,477
123.

677
00:48:43,945 --> 00:48:45,821
On your feet.

678
00:48:47,532 --> 00:48:49,408
Face the wall.

679
00:49:23,902 --> 00:49:25,986
HADLEY: Turn around and face the Warden.

680
00:49:31,701 --> 00:49:33,786
Pleased to see you reading this.

681
00:49:35,163 --> 00:49:37,456
Any favorite passages?

682
00:49:38,500 --> 00:49:43,629
"Watch ye, therefore, for ye know not
when the master of the house cometh."

683
00:49:44,673 --> 00:49:47,550
Mark 13:35.

684
00:49:47,634 --> 00:49:50,302
I always liked that one.

685
00:49:50,387 --> 00:49:51,887
But I prefer...

686
00:49:51,972 --> 00:49:54,723
"I'm the light of the world.

687
00:49:54,808 --> 00:49:58,352
He that followeth me shall not walk in darkness,
but shall have the light of life."

688
00:49:58,436 --> 00:50:01,021
John, Chapter 8, Verse 12.

689
00:50:01,106 --> 00:50:04,567
I hear you're good with numbers. How nice.

690
00:50:05,694 --> 00:50:07,778
A man should have a skill.

691
00:50:09,865 --> 00:50:11,323
Wanna explain this?

692
00:50:12,409 --> 00:50:15,870
It's called a rock blanket.
It's for shaping and polishing rocks.

693
00:50:16,913 --> 00:50:18,873
A little hobby of mine.

694
00:50:29,718 --> 00:50:32,720
It's pretty clean. Some contraband here,

695
00:50:32,804 --> 00:50:34,889
but nothing to get in a twist over.

696
00:50:38,727 --> 00:50:40,811
I can't say I approve of this.

697
00:50:42,314 --> 00:50:44,023
But I suppose...

698
00:50:45,984 --> 00:50:47,860
..exceptions can be made.

699
00:50:58,246 --> 00:51:00,539
HADLEY: Lock 'em up!

700
00:51:02,542 --> 00:51:04,501
I almost forgot.

701
00:51:04,586 --> 00:51:06,837
I'd hate to deprive you of this.

702
00:51:06,922 --> 00:51:08,380
Salvation lies within.

703
00:51:09,466 --> 00:51:10,799
Yes, sir.

704
00:51:19,100 --> 00:51:21,685
RED: Tossing cells was just an excuse.

705
00:51:22,229 --> 00:51:25,814
The truth is, Norton wanted to size Andy up.

706
00:51:39,454 --> 00:51:42,581
My wife made that in church group.

707
00:51:44,918 --> 00:51:46,252
Very nice, sir.

708
00:51:47,420 --> 00:51:49,296
Do you enjoy working in the laundry?

709
00:51:50,924 --> 00:51:52,633
No, sir, not especially.

710
00:51:52,717 --> 00:51:55,511
Perhaps we can find something more...

711
00:51:55,595 --> 00:51:57,680
befitting a man of your education.

712
00:52:06,022 --> 00:52:07,690
Hey, Jake. Where's Brooks?

713
00:52:07,774 --> 00:52:09,066
Andy.

714
00:52:09,150 --> 00:52:11,443
I thought I heard you out here!

715
00:52:12,946 --> 00:52:14,530
I've been reassigned to you.

716
00:52:14,614 --> 00:52:17,783
I know. They told me. (Chuckles)

717
00:52:17,867 --> 00:52:19,952
Ain't that a kick in the head?

718
00:52:20,036 --> 00:52:22,538
Well, I'll give you the dime tour.

719
00:52:22,622 --> 00:52:24,707
Come on.

720
00:52:24,791 --> 00:52:26,959
Well, here she is.

721
00:52:27,043 --> 00:52:29,586
Shawshank Prison library.

722
00:52:29,671 --> 00:52:32,548
National Geographics.

723
00:52:32,632 --> 00:52:35,467
Reader's Digest condensed books.

724
00:52:35,552 --> 00:52:37,845
Louis L'Amour.

725
00:52:37,846 --> 00:52:39,972
Look Magazine.

726
00:52:40,056 --> 00:52:42,558
Erle Stanley Gardners.

727
00:52:42,642 --> 00:52:46,186
Every evening,
I load up the cart and make my round.

728
00:52:46,187 --> 00:52:49,356
I enter the names on this clipboard here.

729
00:52:49,441 --> 00:52:52,901
Easy-peasy, Japanesey. Any questions?

730
00:52:52,986 --> 00:52:55,946
Brooks, how long have you been librarian?

731
00:52:56,031 --> 00:53:01,201
Oh...I come here in '05,
and they made me librarian in 1912.

732
00:53:01,286 --> 00:53:04,663
And in all that time,
have you ever had an assistant?

733
00:53:04,748 --> 00:53:07,333
No. No, not much to it, really.

734
00:53:07,417 --> 00:53:09,335
Well, why me? Why now?

735
00:53:09,419 --> 00:53:10,919
I don't know,

736
00:53:11,004 --> 00:53:14,340
but it'll be nice to have some company
down here for a change.

737
00:53:14,341 --> 00:53:16,425
HADLEY: Dufresne!

738
00:53:25,727 --> 00:53:27,811
That's him. That's the one.

739
00:53:35,362 --> 00:53:36,653
I'm Dekins.

740
00:53:36,738 --> 00:53:39,198
I was er...

741
00:53:39,282 --> 00:53:43,285
thinking about maybe setting up
some kind of trust fund for my kids' educations.

742
00:53:45,622 --> 00:53:47,081
Oh...

743
00:53:47,165 --> 00:53:48,832
I see.

744
00:53:50,293 --> 00:53:52,169
Well, erm...

745
00:53:53,546 --> 00:53:55,089
..why don't we have a seat,

746
00:53:55,173 --> 00:53:57,257
and talk it over?

747
00:53:59,803 --> 00:54:02,721
Brooks,
do you have a piece of paper and a pencil?

748
00:54:14,192 --> 00:54:15,901
Thanks.

749
00:54:15,985 --> 00:54:17,653
So...

750
00:54:18,905 --> 00:54:21,407
Mr. Dekins...

751
00:54:21,491 --> 00:54:25,953
And then Andy says to Mr. Dekins,
"Do you want your sons to go to Harvard,

752
00:54:26,037 --> 00:54:28,163
- or Yale?"
- He didn't say that?

753
00:54:28,248 --> 00:54:30,916
As God is my witness, he did!

754
00:54:31,000 --> 00:54:34,378
Dekins just blinked for a second.
Then he laughed himself silly.

755
00:54:34,462 --> 00:54:36,338
And afterwards he shook Andy's hand.

756
00:54:36,423 --> 00:54:37,798
My ass!

757
00:54:37,882 --> 00:54:39,091
Shook his hand.

758
00:54:39,175 --> 00:54:42,136
Hell, I...I near soiled myself! (Laughs)

759
00:54:42,220 --> 00:54:46,432
All Andy needed was a suit and a tie
and a little jiggly hula girl on his desk,

760
00:54:46,516 --> 00:54:47,850
and he would have been:

761
00:54:47,934 --> 00:54:49,476
"Mr. Dufresne, if you please"!

762
00:54:49,561 --> 00:54:51,311
Making a few friends, Andy?

763
00:54:52,355 --> 00:54:54,314
I wouldn't say "friends".

764
00:54:54,399 --> 00:54:58,402
I'm a convicted murderer
who provides sound financial planning.

765
00:54:58,486 --> 00:55:00,821
It's a wonderful pet to have.

766
00:55:00,905 --> 00:55:02,823
Got you out of the laundry, though.

767
00:55:02,907 --> 00:55:05,117
Well, it might do more than that.

768
00:55:05,201 --> 00:55:08,203
How about expanding the library?
Get some new books in there.

769
00:55:08,288 --> 00:55:10,998
If you're gonna ask for something,
ask for a pool table.

770
00:55:11,082 --> 00:55:13,542
- Right.
- How do you expect to do that?

771
00:55:13,626 --> 00:55:18,881
I mean, get new books in here,
Mr. Dufresne, if you please?

772
00:55:18,965 --> 00:55:20,716
Ask the Warden for funds.

773
00:55:22,135 --> 00:55:25,429
Son, son. Six wardens
have been through here in my tenure,

774
00:55:25,513 --> 00:55:29,141
and I've learned one immutable, universal truth.

775
00:55:29,225 --> 00:55:30,934
Not one of them born...

776
00:55:30,935 --> 00:55:35,898
whose asshole wouldn't pucker up tighter than
a snare drum when you asked them for funds.

777
00:55:35,982 --> 00:55:38,275
The budget's stretched thin, as it is.

778
00:55:38,359 --> 00:55:39,610
I see.

779
00:55:39,694 --> 00:55:43,238
Perhaps I could write to the State Senate
and request funds from them.

780
00:55:43,323 --> 00:55:47,701
As far as they're concerned, there's only three
ways to spend money when it comes to prisons:

781
00:55:47,785 --> 00:55:49,870
more walls, more bars and more guards.

782
00:55:49,954 --> 00:55:54,374
Still, I'd like to try, with your permission.
A letter a week. They can't ignore me forever.

783
00:55:54,459 --> 00:55:56,001
Sure can.

784
00:55:56,085 --> 00:55:58,962
But you write your letters, if it makes you happy.

785
00:55:59,047 --> 00:56:01,340
I'll even mail them for you. How's that?

786
00:56:03,259 --> 00:56:06,303
RED: So, Andy started writing a letter a week,

787
00:56:06,387 --> 00:56:08,472
just like he said.

788
00:56:12,519 --> 00:56:16,647
And, just like Norton said, Andy got no answers.

789
00:56:24,489 --> 00:56:29,993
The following April, Andy did tax returns
for half the guards at Shawshank.

790
00:56:30,078 --> 00:56:33,163
The year after that, he did them all...

791
00:56:33,248 --> 00:56:35,332
including the Warden's.

792
00:56:36,709 --> 00:56:40,462
The year after that, they rescheduled
the start of the intramural season

793
00:56:40,547 --> 00:56:42,631
to coincide with tax season.

794
00:56:44,926 --> 00:56:49,137
The guards on the opposing teams
all remembered to bring their W-2s.

795
00:56:49,222 --> 00:56:53,350
So, Moresby Prison issued you your gun,
but you had to pay for it?

796
00:56:53,434 --> 00:56:55,185
Damn right. The holster, too.

797
00:56:55,270 --> 00:56:56,520
That's tax-deductible.

798
00:56:56,604 --> 00:56:57,771
You can write that off.

799
00:56:57,855 --> 00:57:01,191
RED: Yes, sir.
Andy was a regular cottage industry.

800
00:57:01,276 --> 00:57:04,319
In fact, it got so busy at tax time...

801
00:57:04,404 --> 00:57:06,446
he was allowed a staff.

802
00:57:06,531 --> 00:57:08,991
Hey, Red, can you hand me a stack of 1040s?

803
00:57:09,075 --> 00:57:11,743
It got me out of the wood shop
a month out of the year,

804
00:57:11,828 --> 00:57:14,496
and that was fine by me.

805
00:57:14,581 --> 00:57:17,958
And still...he kept sending those letters.

806
00:57:23,256 --> 00:57:25,090
Red, Andy.

807
00:57:25,174 --> 00:57:26,383
It's Brooks.

808
00:57:26,467 --> 00:57:28,927
- Watch the door.

809
00:57:29,012 --> 00:57:30,596
Please, Brooks.

810
00:57:30,680 --> 00:57:32,681
- Just calm the fuck down.
- Stay back!

811
00:57:32,765 --> 00:57:34,808
- Stay back!
- What the hell's going on?

812
00:57:34,892 --> 00:57:37,978
You tell me. One second he's fine.
Then out comes the knife.

813
00:57:38,062 --> 00:57:40,522
Brooks, we can talk about this. Right?

814
00:57:40,607 --> 00:57:42,733
Nothing to talk about, goddamn it!

815
00:57:42,817 --> 00:57:44,526
I'm gonna cut his fucking throat.

816
00:57:44,611 --> 00:57:46,486
Heywood? Wait, what's he done to you?

817
00:57:46,571 --> 00:57:48,697
It's what they done!

818
00:57:48,781 --> 00:57:50,198
I...I got no choice...

819
00:57:50,283 --> 00:57:52,951
You're not gonna hurt him.
Even Heywood knows that.

820
00:57:53,036 --> 00:57:55,662
- Right, Heywood?
- I...I know that, sure.

821
00:57:55,747 --> 00:57:59,166
You won't hurt him, because he's a friend,
and you're a reasonable man.

822
00:57:59,250 --> 00:58:00,542
That's right. Right, guys?

823
00:58:00,627 --> 00:58:04,004
- Yes.
- So, put the knife down. Brooks, look at me.

824
00:58:04,088 --> 00:58:05,922
Put the knife down.

825
00:58:07,008 --> 00:58:08,175
Brooks...

826
00:58:08,259 --> 00:58:10,510
Look at his neck, for God's sake.

827
00:58:10,595 --> 00:58:12,929
Brooks, look at his neck. It's bleeding.

828
00:58:14,265 --> 00:58:15,849
It's the only...

829
00:58:15,933 --> 00:58:18,226
It's the only way they'd let me stay.

830
00:58:18,311 --> 00:58:20,896
Come on. This is crazy.
You don't want to do this.

831
00:58:20,897 --> 00:58:22,981
Put it down.

832
00:58:30,365 --> 00:58:35,077
Come on. Take it easy.
You're going to be all right.

833
00:58:35,161 --> 00:58:36,536
HEYWOOD: Him?

834
00:58:36,621 --> 00:58:40,290
Hell, what about me?
Crazy old fool damn near cut my throat.

835
00:58:40,375 --> 00:58:43,001
RED: Shit, Heywood,
you've had worse from shaving.

836
00:58:43,086 --> 00:58:45,337
What the hell did you do to set him off, anyway?

837
00:58:45,421 --> 00:58:47,756
Nothing. I come in here to say fare-thee-well.

838
00:58:47,840 --> 00:58:50,967
Ain't you heard? His parole's come through.

839
00:58:53,012 --> 00:58:56,139
I just don't understand what happened in there.
That's all.

840
00:58:56,224 --> 00:58:58,809
The old man's as crazy
as a rat in a tin shithouse.

841
00:58:58,893 --> 00:59:01,103
Oh, Heywood, that's enough out of you.

842
00:59:01,187 --> 00:59:04,189
- I heard he had you shitting in your pants.
- Fuck you!

843
00:59:04,273 --> 00:59:05,649
Would you knock it off?

844
00:59:05,733 --> 00:59:08,068
Brooks ain't no bug.

845
00:59:08,152 --> 00:59:10,028
He's just...

846
00:59:11,906 --> 00:59:13,740
He's just institutionalized.

847
00:59:15,076 --> 00:59:16,743
Institutionalized, my ass.

848
00:59:16,828 --> 00:59:21,123
The man's been in here 50 years, Heywood.
50 years.

849
00:59:21,207 --> 00:59:22,916
This is all he knows.

850
00:59:23,000 --> 00:59:25,419
In here, he's an important man.

851
00:59:25,503 --> 00:59:26,962
He's an educated man.

852
00:59:27,046 --> 00:59:29,131
Outside, he's nothing.

853
00:59:31,300 --> 00:59:33,927
Just a used-up con with arthritis in both hands.

854
00:59:34,011 --> 00:59:37,222
Probably couldn't get a library card if he tried.

855
00:59:37,306 --> 00:59:39,391
You know what I'm trying to say?

856
00:59:39,475 --> 00:59:42,227
Red, I do believe you're talking out of your ass.

857
00:59:43,479 --> 00:59:46,398
You believe whatever you want, Floyd.

858
00:59:46,399 --> 00:59:48,942
But I'm telling you, these walls are funny.

859
00:59:50,361 --> 00:59:52,446
First you hate 'em...

860
00:59:53,990 --> 00:59:56,074
..then you get used to 'em.

861
00:59:57,702 --> 01:00:00,328
Enough time passes...

862
01:00:00,413 --> 01:00:03,373
you get so you depend on 'em.

863
01:00:03,458 --> 01:00:05,792
That's institutionalized.

864
01:00:05,877 --> 01:00:07,252
JIGGER: Shit.

865
01:00:07,253 --> 01:00:08,879
You can never get like that.

866
01:00:08,963 --> 01:00:10,881
Oh, yeah?

867
01:00:10,965 --> 01:00:13,550
Say that when you've been here
as long as Brooks has.

868
01:00:13,634 --> 01:00:15,510
Goddamn right.

869
01:00:17,847 --> 01:00:20,390
They send you here for life.

870
01:00:20,475 --> 01:00:22,559
That's exactly what they take.

871
01:00:24,479 --> 01:00:26,563
The part that counts, anyway.

872
01:00:34,071 --> 01:00:36,239
I can't take care of you no more, Jake.

873
01:00:38,159 --> 01:00:39,993
You go on, now.

874
01:00:41,078 --> 01:00:45,749
You're free.

875
01:01:01,641 --> 01:01:03,517
Bye.

876
01:01:04,602 --> 01:01:06,520
Good luck, Brooks.

877
01:01:43,099 --> 01:01:45,809
BROOKS: Dear fellas...

878
01:01:45,893 --> 01:01:50,105
I can't believe
how fast things move on the outside.

879
01:01:51,524 --> 01:01:54,568
Watch it, old-timer! Are you trying to get killed?

880
01:01:54,652 --> 01:01:57,988
I saw an automobile once, when I was a kid,

881
01:01:58,072 --> 01:02:00,991
but now...they're everywhere.

882
01:02:05,204 --> 01:02:08,790
The world went and got itself
in a big damn hurry.

883
01:02:25,141 --> 01:02:31,438
The parole board got me into
this halfway house called The Brewer,

884
01:02:31,522 --> 01:02:35,317
and a job bagging groceries at the Foodway.

885
01:02:37,361 --> 01:02:42,699
It's hard work, and I try to keep up,
but my hands hurt most of the time.

886
01:02:42,783 --> 01:02:44,784
WOMAN: Make sure your man double-bags.

887
01:02:44,869 --> 01:02:47,120
Last time, the bottom near came out.

888
01:02:47,204 --> 01:02:50,498
Make sure you double-bag, like the lady says.
Understand?

889
01:02:50,583 --> 01:02:52,042
Yes, sir. I surely will.

890
01:02:52,126 --> 01:02:55,921
BROOKS: I don't think
the store manager likes me very much.

891
01:02:58,883 --> 01:03:03,678
Sometimes, after work,
I go to the park and feed the birds.

892
01:03:03,763 --> 01:03:09,100
I keep thinking Jake might just show up
and say hello.

893
01:03:09,185 --> 01:03:10,977
But he never does.

894
01:03:12,063 --> 01:03:17,442
And I hope, wherever he is,
he's doing OK and making new friends.

895
01:03:20,947 --> 01:03:23,865
I have trouble sleeping at night.

896
01:03:23,950 --> 01:03:26,743
I have bad dreams, like I'm falling.

897
01:03:27,828 --> 01:03:29,746
I wake up, scared.

898
01:03:29,830 --> 01:03:33,833
Sometimes it takes me a while
to remember where I am.

899
01:03:35,461 --> 01:03:36,920
Maybe I should get me a gun

900
01:03:37,004 --> 01:03:40,882
and rob the Foodway, so they'd send me home.

901
01:03:40,967 --> 01:03:43,301
I could shoot the manager, while I was at it.

902
01:03:43,386 --> 01:03:45,345
Sort of like a bonus.

903
01:03:46,847 --> 01:03:51,101
I guess I'm too old for that sort of nonsense
any more.

904
01:03:51,185 --> 01:03:53,228
I don't like it here.

905
01:03:53,312 --> 01:03:55,981
I'm tired of being afraid all the time.

906
01:03:56,065 --> 01:03:57,148
I've decided...

907
01:03:57,233 --> 01:03:59,109
not to stay.

908
01:04:10,454 --> 01:04:13,957
I doubt they'll kick up any fuss.

909
01:04:14,041 --> 01:04:16,126
Not for an old crook like me.

910
01:05:19,148 --> 01:05:23,193
"I doubt they'll kick up any fuss.
Not for an old crook like me.

911
01:05:24,361 --> 01:05:28,406
PS. Tell Heywood
I'm sorry I put a knife to his throat.

912
01:05:28,407 --> 01:05:30,492
No hard feelings. Brooks."

913
01:05:41,212 --> 01:05:43,296
He should have died in here.

914
01:05:48,719 --> 01:05:53,181
HADLEY: What the fuck have you done?
It's a goddamn mess. I'll tell you that.

915
01:05:57,895 --> 01:05:59,312
What's all this?

916
01:05:59,396 --> 01:06:03,274
You tell me, fuck-stick.
They're all addressed to you.

917
01:06:03,359 --> 01:06:04,651
Take it.

918
01:06:13,619 --> 01:06:15,203
"Dear Mr. Dufresne...

919
01:06:16,288 --> 01:06:18,289
In response to your repeated enquiries,

920
01:06:18,374 --> 01:06:23,086
the State has allocated the enclosed funds
for your library project."

921
01:06:23,170 --> 01:06:24,546
This is 200 dollars.

922
01:06:25,589 --> 01:06:29,509
"In addition, the Library District
has generously responded

923
01:06:29,593 --> 01:06:32,303
with a charitable donation
of used books and sundries.

924
01:06:32,388 --> 01:06:34,472
We trust this will fill your needs.

925
01:06:34,557 --> 01:06:38,685
We now consider the matter closed.
Please stop sending us letters."

926
01:06:38,769 --> 01:06:41,604
I want all this cleared out
before the Warden gets back.

927
01:06:41,689 --> 01:06:43,356
Yes, sir.

928
01:06:47,111 --> 01:06:48,486
Good for you, Andy.

929
01:06:51,115 --> 01:06:52,574
Wow!

930
01:06:53,659 --> 01:06:55,743
It only took six years.

931
01:06:57,037 --> 01:06:59,914
From now on,
I'll write two letters a week, instead of one.

932
01:06:59,999 --> 01:07:03,543
Oh, I believe you're crazy enough.
You'd better get all this stuff out,

933
01:07:03,627 --> 01:07:04,794
like the Captain said.

934
01:07:04,879 --> 01:07:08,756
Now, I'm gonna go pinch a loaf.
When I come back, this is all gone, right?

935
01:07:14,930 --> 01:07:16,931
Wow.

936
01:07:47,004 --> 01:07:49,714
♪ MOZART:
Duettino from The Marriage Of Figaro

937
01:07:51,175 --> 01:07:53,760
♪ Sull'aria...

938
01:07:57,389 --> 01:07:58,389
Andy,

939
01:07:58,474 --> 01:07:59,807
do you hear that?

940
01:07:59,892 --> 01:08:03,603
♪ ..zeffiretto

941
01:08:15,824 --> 01:08:23,665
♪ Questa sera spirera

942
01:08:54,071 --> 01:08:58,366
♪ Sotto i pini

943
01:08:58,450 --> 01:09:03,663
♪ Del boschetto

944
01:09:05,165 --> 01:09:07,542
♪ Sotto i pini...

945
01:09:07,626 --> 01:09:10,503
Dufresne?

946
01:09:10,587 --> 01:09:12,171
Dufresne?

947
01:09:12,256 --> 01:09:14,340
Andy, let me out.

948
01:09:17,052 --> 01:09:20,596
Andy?

949
01:09:20,681 --> 01:09:25,852
RED: I have no idea to this day
what those two Italian ladies were singing about.

950
01:09:25,936 --> 01:09:28,354
The truth is, I don't want to know.

951
01:09:28,439 --> 01:09:30,523
Some things are best left unsaid.

952
01:09:34,570 --> 01:09:39,615
I like to think they were singing about something
so beautiful, it can't be expressed in words...

953
01:09:39,700 --> 01:09:42,076
and makes your heart ache because of it.

954
01:09:44,288 --> 01:09:46,998
I tell you, those voices soared...

955
01:09:47,082 --> 01:09:51,627
higher and farther
than anybody in a grey place dares to dream.

956
01:09:51,712 --> 01:09:55,089
It was like some beautiful bird
flapped into our drab little cage

957
01:09:55,174 --> 01:09:58,301
and made those walls dissolve away.

958
01:09:58,385 --> 01:10:00,720
And for the briefest of moments...

959
01:10:00,804 --> 01:10:03,890
every last man at Shawshank felt free.

960
01:10:06,393 --> 01:10:08,895
It pissed the Warden off something awful.

961
01:10:10,314 --> 01:10:11,439
Open the door.

962
01:10:14,193 --> 01:10:16,110
Open it up!

963
01:10:17,321 --> 01:10:19,697
Dufresne, open this door!

964
01:10:21,325 --> 01:10:22,658
Turn that off!

965
01:10:29,458 --> 01:10:32,210
I am warning you, Dufresne. Turn that off!

966
01:10:46,058 --> 01:10:47,934
Dufresne...

967
01:10:49,061 --> 01:10:51,145
You're mine now.

968
01:10:58,695 --> 01:11:01,447
RED: Andy got two weeks in the hole
for that little stunt.

969
01:11:01,532 --> 01:11:03,950
On your feet!

970
01:11:05,160 --> 01:11:07,245
- Hey, look who's here.
- Maestro!

971
01:11:08,747 --> 01:11:12,792
Y-Y-You couldn't play something good, huh?
Hank Williams or something?

972
01:11:12,876 --> 01:11:15,545
They broke the door down
before I could take requests.

973
01:11:15,629 --> 01:11:17,422
Was it worth it, two weeks in the hole?

974
01:11:17,506 --> 01:11:18,756
Easiest time I ever did.

975
01:11:18,841 --> 01:11:21,884
Bullshit!
There's no such thing as easy time in the hole.

976
01:11:21,885 --> 01:11:24,512
A week in the hole is like a year.

977
01:11:24,596 --> 01:11:27,306
I had Mr. Mozart to keep me company.

978
01:11:27,391 --> 01:11:30,726
So, they let you tote that record player
down there, huh?

979
01:11:32,855 --> 01:11:34,480
It was in here.

980
01:11:34,565 --> 01:11:36,232
And in here.

981
01:11:37,443 --> 01:11:41,696
That's the beauty of music.
They can't get that from you.

982
01:11:45,617 --> 01:11:48,119
Haven't you ever felt that way about music?

983
01:11:49,788 --> 01:11:53,875
Well, I played a mean harmonica,
as a younger man.

984
01:11:53,959 --> 01:11:55,751
Lost interest in it, though.

985
01:11:55,836 --> 01:11:57,670
Didn't make much sense in here.

986
01:11:57,754 --> 01:12:00,214
Here's where it makes the most sense.

987
01:12:00,299 --> 01:12:02,884
You need it, so you don't forget.

988
01:12:02,968 --> 01:12:04,302
Forget?

989
01:12:04,386 --> 01:12:06,345
Forget that...

990
01:12:06,430 --> 01:12:08,514
there are places...

991
01:12:09,850 --> 01:12:12,393
..in the world that aren't made out of stone...

992
01:12:12,478 --> 01:12:14,520
that there's a...

993
01:12:14,605 --> 01:12:16,439
there's something...

994
01:12:16,523 --> 01:12:19,317
inside that they can't get to...

995
01:12:19,401 --> 01:12:21,694
that they can't touch.

996
01:12:21,778 --> 01:12:23,654
It's yours.

997
01:12:25,073 --> 01:12:26,782
What are you talking about?

998
01:12:27,868 --> 01:12:29,452
Hope.

999
01:12:29,536 --> 01:12:31,412
Hope?

1000
01:12:33,248 --> 01:12:35,917
Let me tell you something, my friend.

1001
01:12:36,001 --> 01:12:37,960
Hope is a dangerous thing.

1002
01:12:39,546 --> 01:12:41,589
Hope can drive a man insane.

1003
01:12:42,633 --> 01:12:44,550
It's got no use on the inside.

1004
01:12:44,635 --> 01:12:47,303
You'd better get used to that idea.

1005
01:12:49,723 --> 01:12:51,349
Like Brooks did?

1006
01:13:16,583 --> 01:13:17,625
Sit down.

1007
01:13:22,839 --> 01:13:26,300
It says here that you've served
30 years of a life sentence.

1008
01:13:26,385 --> 01:13:29,011
You feel you've been rehabilitated?

1009
01:13:29,096 --> 01:13:31,180
Oh, yes, sir.

1010
01:13:31,181 --> 01:13:33,266
Without a doubt.

1011
01:13:34,309 --> 01:13:36,644
And I can honestly say I'm a changed man.

1012
01:13:39,231 --> 01:13:41,274
No danger to society here.

1013
01:13:41,358 --> 01:13:43,442
God's honest truth.

1014
01:13:45,779 --> 01:13:47,863
Absolutely rehabilitated.

1015
01:13:57,791 --> 01:13:59,875
30 years.

1016
01:14:00,961 --> 01:14:03,004
Jesus, when they say it like that...

1017
01:14:03,088 --> 01:14:05,172
You wonder where it went.

1018
01:14:08,135 --> 01:14:10,219
I wonder where ten years went.

1019
01:14:15,267 --> 01:14:16,559
Here.

1020
01:14:16,643 --> 01:14:18,811
Little parole rejection present.

1021
01:14:20,647 --> 01:14:22,690
Go ahead and open it.

1022
01:14:22,774 --> 01:14:26,569
I had to go through one of your competitors.
I hope you don't mind.

1023
01:14:26,653 --> 01:14:28,738
I wanted it to be a surprise.

1024
01:14:38,874 --> 01:14:40,958
It's very pretty, Andy.

1025
01:14:42,044 --> 01:14:44,128
Thank you.

1026
01:14:45,881 --> 01:14:47,965
Are you going to play it?

1027
01:14:52,095 --> 01:14:54,180
No.

1028
01:14:56,475 --> 01:14:58,559
Not right now.

1029
01:15:05,817 --> 01:15:07,902
Tier 3 South. Clear.

1030
01:15:08,945 --> 01:15:10,988
Tier 4 North. Clear.

1031
01:15:11,073 --> 01:15:13,157
Tier 1 South. Clear.

1032
01:15:15,994 --> 01:15:18,079
Fall in!

1033
01:15:52,656 --> 01:15:54,240
Lights out!

1034
01:16:29,151 --> 01:16:31,193
RED: Andy was as good as his word.

1035
01:16:31,278 --> 01:16:33,779
He wrote two letters a week instead of one.

1036
01:16:36,408 --> 01:16:40,536
In 1959, the State Senate
finally clued into the fact

1037
01:16:40,620 --> 01:16:43,497
they couldn't buy him off
with just a $200 check.

1038
01:16:43,582 --> 01:16:50,171
Appropriations Committee voted
an annual payment of $500 just to shut him up.

1039
01:16:50,255 --> 01:16:51,964
And you'd be amazed

1040
01:16:52,048 --> 01:16:53,716
how far Andy could stretch it.

1041
01:16:53,800 --> 01:16:56,677
He made deals with book clubs, charity groups.

1042
01:16:56,762 --> 01:16:59,138
He bought remaindered books by the pound.

1043
01:16:59,222 --> 01:17:01,432
Treasure Island.

1044
01:17:01,516 --> 01:17:03,684
Robert Louis...

1045
01:17:03,769 --> 01:17:04,977
..Stevenson.

1046
01:17:05,061 --> 01:17:06,520
Fiction, adventure.

1047
01:17:08,523 --> 01:17:10,149
What's next?

1048
01:17:10,150 --> 01:17:12,860
I got here Auto Repair and...

1049
01:17:13,904 --> 01:17:15,237
..Soap Carving.

1050
01:17:15,322 --> 01:17:18,699
Trade skills and hobbies. Goes under
Educational. The stack behind you.

1051
01:17:18,784 --> 01:17:20,826
The Count Of Monte Crisco.

1052
01:17:20,911 --> 01:17:23,245
That's Cristo, you dumb shit.

1053
01:17:23,330 --> 01:17:27,124
By Alexandree...Dumass.

1054
01:17:27,209 --> 01:17:32,129
Dumb-ass?

1055
01:17:34,633 --> 01:17:36,967
Dumas. You know what that's about?

1056
01:17:37,052 --> 01:17:38,427
Uh-uh.

1057
01:17:38,512 --> 01:17:41,222
You'll like it. It's about a prison break.

1058
01:17:41,306 --> 01:17:45,309
We ought to file that under Educational, too,
oughtn't we?

1059
01:17:45,393 --> 01:17:49,563
RED: The rest of us did our best to pitch in
when and where we could.

1060
01:17:50,816 --> 01:17:52,650
By the year Kennedy was shot,

1061
01:17:52,734 --> 01:17:57,071
Andy had transformed a storage room
smelling of rat turds and turpentine

1062
01:17:57,072 --> 01:18:00,407
into the best prison library in New England,

1063
01:18:00,492 --> 01:18:04,370
complete with a fine selection of Hank Williams.

1064
01:18:04,454 --> 01:18:08,624
♪ Lawd I don't know what I'll do-ooo

1065
01:18:08,708 --> 01:18:11,460
♪ All I do is sit and sigh...

1066
01:18:11,545 --> 01:18:16,298
RED: That was also the year Warden Norton
instituted his famous Inside Out program.

1067
01:18:16,383 --> 01:18:19,093
You may remember reading about it.

1068
01:18:19,177 --> 01:18:22,596
It made all the papers
and got his picture in Look Magazine.

1069
01:18:22,681 --> 01:18:24,348
No free ride.

1070
01:18:24,432 --> 01:18:27,393
But rather a genuine, progressive advance

1071
01:18:27,477 --> 01:18:29,645
in corrections and rehabilitation.

1072
01:18:30,689 --> 01:18:33,023
Our inmates, properly supervised,

1073
01:18:33,108 --> 01:18:35,860
will be put to work outside these walls,

1074
01:18:35,944 --> 01:18:38,028
performing all manner of public service.

1075
01:18:38,113 --> 01:18:42,032
These men can learn
the value of an honest day's labor,

1076
01:18:42,117 --> 01:18:45,786
while providing a valuable service
to the community and...

1077
01:18:45,871 --> 01:18:51,083
at a bare minimum of expense
to Mr. and Mrs. John Q Taxpayer.

1078
01:18:51,167 --> 01:18:53,836
RED: Of course,
Norton failed to mention to the press

1079
01:18:53,920 --> 01:18:57,214
that "bare minimum of expense"
is a fairly loose term.

1080
01:18:57,299 --> 01:19:00,467
There are a hundred different ways
to skim off the top.

1081
01:19:00,552 --> 01:19:03,178
Men, materials, you name it.

1082
01:19:04,222 --> 01:19:06,974
And, oh, my Lord, how the money rolled in.

1083
01:19:07,058 --> 01:19:09,184
If this keeps up, you'll put me out of business.

1084
01:19:09,269 --> 01:19:11,437
- Ned!
- This pool of slave labor you've got,

1085
01:19:11,521 --> 01:19:13,564
you can underbid any contractor in town.

1086
01:19:13,648 --> 01:19:17,610
Ned, we're providing
a valuable community service here.

1087
01:19:17,694 --> 01:19:19,945
Well, that's fine for the papers

1088
01:19:20,030 --> 01:19:21,530
but I've got a family to feed.

1089
01:19:21,615 --> 01:19:23,324
Sam...

1090
01:19:23,408 --> 01:19:25,868
Sam, we go back a long way.

1091
01:19:25,952 --> 01:19:28,287
I need this new highway contract.

1092
01:19:28,371 --> 01:19:30,581
I don't get it, and I go under.

1093
01:19:30,665 --> 01:19:32,708
That's a fact.

1094
01:19:32,792 --> 01:19:34,877
You just have some of this fine pie

1095
01:19:34,961 --> 01:19:37,004
my missus made especially for you,

1096
01:19:37,088 --> 01:19:38,422
and you think about that.

1097
01:19:46,473 --> 01:19:49,391
Ned, I wouldn't worry too much
about this contract.

1098
01:19:49,476 --> 01:19:52,686
It seems to me I already got
my boys committed elsewhere.

1099
01:19:54,564 --> 01:19:57,149
You be sure and thank Maisie for this fine pie.

1100
01:19:58,777 --> 01:20:00,945
RED: And behind every shady deal,

1101
01:20:01,029 --> 01:20:03,238
behind every dollar earned,

1102
01:20:03,323 --> 01:20:06,492
there was Andy, keeping the books.

1103
01:20:07,535 --> 01:20:10,955
Two deposits.
Maine National and New England First.

1104
01:20:11,039 --> 01:20:13,123
Night drops as always, sir.

1105
01:20:43,530 --> 01:20:45,280
Get my stuff down to the laundry.

1106
01:20:45,365 --> 01:20:48,158
- Two suits for dry-clean and a bag of whatnot.
- Yes, sir.

1107
01:20:48,243 --> 01:20:51,996
Tell them if they over-starch my shirts again,
they'll hear about it from me.

1108
01:20:52,080 --> 01:20:54,081
How do I look?

1109
01:20:54,165 --> 01:20:56,875
- Very nice, sir.
- Big charity to-do up Portland way.

1110
01:20:56,960 --> 01:20:58,669
The Governor's going to be there.

1111
01:21:00,088 --> 01:21:02,131
Want the rest of this?

1112
01:21:02,215 --> 01:21:04,299
The woman can't bake worth shit.

1113
01:21:05,677 --> 01:21:07,469
Thank you, sir.

1114
01:21:09,639 --> 01:21:13,392
- He's got his fingers in a lot of pies, I hear.
- What you hear isn't half of it.

1115
01:21:13,476 --> 01:21:15,936
He's got scams you haven't even dreamed of.

1116
01:21:16,021 --> 01:21:18,063
Kickbacks on his kickbacks.

1117
01:21:18,148 --> 01:21:21,025
There's a river of dirty money
running through this place.

1118
01:21:21,109 --> 01:21:24,361
But the problem with having all that money
is you'll have to explain

1119
01:21:24,446 --> 01:21:27,031
- where it came from.
- That's where I come in.

1120
01:21:27,115 --> 01:21:29,616
I channel it, filter it, funnel it.

1121
01:21:29,701 --> 01:21:32,244
Stocks, securities, tax-free municipals.

1122
01:21:32,328 --> 01:21:34,997
I send that money out into the real world.

1123
01:21:35,081 --> 01:21:38,709
- And when it comes back...
- Clean as a virgin's honey pot, huh?

1124
01:21:38,793 --> 01:21:40,002
Cleaner.

1125
01:21:40,086 --> 01:21:43,714
By the time Norton retires,
I'll have made him a millionaire.

1126
01:21:46,468 --> 01:21:50,888
If they ever catch on, though, he's gonna
wind up in here wearing a number himself.

1127
01:21:50,972 --> 01:21:54,224
Now, Red, I thought you had
a little more faith in me than that.

1128
01:21:54,309 --> 01:21:57,603
I know you're good, Andy,
but all that paper leaves a trail.

1129
01:21:57,687 --> 01:22:00,689
Now, anybody gets curious,
the FBI, the IRS, whatever,

1130
01:22:00,774 --> 01:22:03,609
it's gonna lead to somebody.

1131
01:22:03,693 --> 01:22:05,652
Sure it is. But not to me.

1132
01:22:05,737 --> 01:22:07,738
And certainly not to the Warden.

1133
01:22:11,785 --> 01:22:13,077
All right. Who?

1134
01:22:13,161 --> 01:22:14,870
Randall Stevens.

1135
01:22:14,954 --> 01:22:16,121
Who?

1136
01:22:17,207 --> 01:22:21,919
The silent silent partner. He's the guilty one,
Your Honor. The man with the bank accounts.

1137
01:22:22,003 --> 01:22:24,171
That's where the filtering process starts.

1138
01:22:24,255 --> 01:22:27,424
If they trace anything,
it's just going to lead to him.

1139
01:22:27,509 --> 01:22:29,093
But who is he?

1140
01:22:29,177 --> 01:22:31,220
He's a phantom, an apparition.

1141
01:22:31,304 --> 01:22:33,263
Second cousin to Harvey the Rabbit.

1142
01:22:33,348 --> 01:22:35,516
I conjured him...

1143
01:22:35,600 --> 01:22:37,643
out of thin air.

1144
01:22:37,727 --> 01:22:40,562
He doesn't exist, except on paper.

1145
01:22:43,691 --> 01:22:46,068
Andy, you can’t just make a person up.

1146
01:22:46,152 --> 01:22:49,279
Sure you can, if you know how
the system works, where the cracks are.

1147
01:22:49,364 --> 01:22:52,866
It's amazing what you can accomplish by mail.

1148
01:22:52,951 --> 01:22:55,577
Mr. Stevens has a birth certificate,

1149
01:22:55,662 --> 01:22:57,996
a driver's license, social security number.

1150
01:22:58,081 --> 01:22:59,289
You're shitting me?

1151
01:22:59,374 --> 01:23:01,333
If they ever trace any of those accounts,

1152
01:23:01,417 --> 01:23:04,294
they're going to wind up chasing
a figment of my imagination.

1153
01:23:04,379 --> 01:23:06,463
Well, I'll be damned.

1154
01:23:07,757 --> 01:23:09,800
Did I say you were good?

1155
01:23:09,884 --> 01:23:12,052
Shit, you're a Rembrandt!

1156
01:23:12,137 --> 01:23:14,179
You know, the funny thing is,

1157
01:23:14,264 --> 01:23:18,225
on the outside, I was an honest man,
straight as an arrow.

1158
01:23:19,269 --> 01:23:21,645
I had to come to prison to be a crook.

1159
01:23:30,405 --> 01:23:31,780
Ever bother you?

1160
01:23:32,824 --> 01:23:36,243
I don't run the scams, Red,
I just process the profits.

1161
01:23:36,327 --> 01:23:38,370
Fine line maybe but...

1162
01:23:38,454 --> 01:23:41,165
I also built that library

1163
01:23:41,249 --> 01:23:43,876
and used it to help a dozen guys
get a high school diploma.

1164
01:23:43,960 --> 01:23:45,836
Why do you think the Warden lets me do it?

1165
01:23:45,920 --> 01:23:48,672
To keep you happy and doing the laundry.

1166
01:23:48,756 --> 01:23:50,883
Money instead of sheets.

1167
01:23:50,967 --> 01:23:53,677
Well, I work cheap. That's the trade-off.

1168
01:23:59,267 --> 01:24:01,476
♪ JOHNNY OTIS: Willy And The Hand Jive

1169
01:24:09,277 --> 01:24:11,069
♪ I know a cat named...

1170
01:24:11,154 --> 01:24:13,822
RED: Tommy Williams
came to Shawshank in 1965

1171
01:24:13,907 --> 01:24:15,949
on a two-year stretch for B&E.

1172
01:24:16,034 --> 01:24:18,160
That's breaking and entering to you.

1173
01:24:18,244 --> 01:24:22,873
The cops caught him sneaking TV sets
out the back door of a JC Penney.

1174
01:24:22,957 --> 01:24:24,708
Young punk.

1175
01:24:24,792 --> 01:24:26,835
Mr. Rock'n'roll.

1176
01:24:26,920 --> 01:24:28,587
Cocky as hell.

1177
01:24:28,671 --> 01:24:31,256
Hey, come on, old boys! Moving like molasses!

1178
01:24:31,341 --> 01:24:33,300
Making me look bad.

1179
01:24:33,301 --> 01:24:35,260
RED: We liked him immediately.

1180
01:24:35,345 --> 01:24:37,387
So I'm backing out the door, right?

1181
01:24:37,472 --> 01:24:39,181
And I got the TV like this.

1182
01:24:39,265 --> 01:24:43,101
It was a big old thing. I couldn't see shit.
Suddenly, I hears this voice.

1183
01:24:43,186 --> 01:24:45,229
"Freeze, kid! Hands in the air."

1184
01:24:45,313 --> 01:24:46,980
Well, I just stand there,

1185
01:24:47,065 --> 01:24:49,858
holding onto that TV. So, finally, the voice says,

1186
01:24:49,943 --> 01:24:51,902
"Do you hear what I said, boy?"

1187
01:24:51,986 --> 01:24:54,363
I say, "Yes, sir, I sure did.

1188
01:24:54,447 --> 01:24:58,075
But if I drop this fucking thing,
you got me on destruction of property, too."

1189
01:25:02,163 --> 01:25:04,873
Hey, you've done a stretch in Cashman, right?

1190
01:25:04,958 --> 01:25:08,794
Yeah. Yeah, that was an easy piece of time,
let me tell you.

1191
01:25:08,878 --> 01:25:11,505
Weekend furloughs, work programs.

1192
01:25:11,589 --> 01:25:13,006
Not like here.

1193
01:25:13,091 --> 01:25:15,425
Sounds like you done time
all over New England.

1194
01:25:15,510 --> 01:25:20,597
Yeah, I've been in and out since I was 13. You
name the place, chances are I've been there.

1195
01:25:20,682 --> 01:25:24,685
Perhaps it's time you tried a new profession?

1196
01:25:24,769 --> 01:25:29,856
What I mean is, you don't seem to be a good
thief. Maybe you should try something else.

1197
01:25:29,941 --> 01:25:33,777
Yeah, well, what the hell
do you know about it, Capone?

1198
01:25:33,861 --> 01:25:35,612
What are you in for?

1199
01:25:35,697 --> 01:25:37,322
Me?

1200
01:25:38,825 --> 01:25:40,909
The lawyer fucked me.

1201
01:25:44,622 --> 01:25:48,041
Everybody's innocent in here.
Don't you know that?

1202
01:25:55,508 --> 01:25:59,678
RED: As it turned out, Tommy had himself
a young wife and a new baby girl.

1203
01:26:00,722 --> 01:26:05,851
Maybe it was the thought of them on the streets
or his child growing up not knowing her daddy.

1204
01:26:05,935 --> 01:26:09,771
Whatever it was,
something lit a fire under that boy's ass.

1205
01:26:13,735 --> 01:26:16,987
I was thinking of maybe
trying for my high school equivalency.

1206
01:26:17,071 --> 01:26:19,656
I hear you helped a couple of fellas with that.

1207
01:26:20,867 --> 01:26:23,076
I don't waste time on losers, Tommy.

1208
01:26:24,662 --> 01:26:26,747
I ain't no goddamn loser.

1209
01:26:28,583 --> 01:26:30,667
- You mean that?
- Yeah.

1210
01:26:32,337 --> 01:26:34,379
You really mean that?

1211
01:26:34,464 --> 01:26:36,131
Yes, sir, I do.

1212
01:26:36,215 --> 01:26:38,342
Good. Because if we do this,

1213
01:26:38,426 --> 01:26:41,178
we do it all the way, 100%, nothing half-assed.

1214
01:26:42,305 --> 01:26:44,389
The thing is, see...

1215
01:26:45,558 --> 01:26:47,392
..I don't read so good.

1216
01:26:48,519 --> 01:26:49,728
Well.

1217
01:26:51,481 --> 01:26:53,523
You don't read...

1218
01:26:53,608 --> 01:26:55,692
so well.

1219
01:26:57,153 --> 01:26:58,820
We'll get to that.

1220
01:26:59,906 --> 01:27:01,656
- R.
- R...

1221
01:27:01,741 --> 01:27:03,116
- S.
- S...

1222
01:27:03,201 --> 01:27:05,369
RED: So Andy took Tommy under his wing.

1223
01:27:05,453 --> 01:27:07,871
He started walking him through his ABCs.

1224
01:27:09,540 --> 01:27:12,125
Tommy took to it pretty well, too.

1225
01:27:12,210 --> 01:27:15,170
The boy found brains he never knew he had.

1226
01:27:17,507 --> 01:27:21,301
Before long, Andy started him
on his course requirements.

1227
01:27:21,386 --> 01:27:23,345
He really liked the kid.

1228
01:27:23,429 --> 01:27:26,598
It gave him a thrill to help a youngster
crawl off the shit heap.

1229
01:27:27,642 --> 01:27:29,684
But that wasn't the only reason.

1230
01:27:29,769 --> 01:27:32,229
Prison time is slow time.

1231
01:27:33,272 --> 01:27:35,524
So you do what you can to keep going.

1232
01:27:35,608 --> 01:27:37,859
Some fellas collect stamps.

1233
01:27:37,860 --> 01:27:40,487
Others build matchstick houses.

1234
01:27:41,531 --> 01:27:43,907
Andy built a library.

1235
01:27:44,951 --> 01:27:46,993
Now he needed a new project.

1236
01:27:47,078 --> 01:27:49,121
Tommy was it.

1237
01:27:49,205 --> 01:27:53,417
It was the same reason he spent years
shaping and polishing those rocks.

1238
01:27:53,501 --> 01:27:57,129
The same reason
he hung his fantasy girlies on the wall.

1239
01:27:58,506 --> 01:28:00,006
In prison,

1240
01:28:00,091 --> 01:28:03,218
a man will do most anything
to keep his mind occupied.

1241
01:28:04,679 --> 01:28:09,141
By 1966, right about the time
Tommy was getting ready to take his exams,

1242
01:28:09,142 --> 01:28:11,184
it was lovely Raquel.

1243
01:28:28,453 --> 01:28:29,494
Time.

1244
01:28:35,501 --> 01:28:36,793
Well?

1245
01:28:37,879 --> 01:28:39,421
Well, it's for shit.

1246
01:28:39,505 --> 01:28:42,007
I wasted a whole fucking year of my time

1247
01:28:42,091 --> 01:28:45,260
- with this bullshit.
- It's probably not as bad as you think.

1248
01:28:45,344 --> 01:28:49,055
It's worse. I didn't get one thing right.
It might as well have been in Chinese.

1249
01:28:49,140 --> 01:28:51,641
Let's see how the score comes out.

1250
01:28:51,726 --> 01:28:54,352
Well, I'll tell you how the goddamn score
comes out.

1251
01:28:56,147 --> 01:28:58,064
Two points right there.

1252
01:28:58,149 --> 01:28:59,941
There's your goddamn score!

1253
01:29:00,026 --> 01:29:03,862
Goddamn cats crawling up trees,
5x5 is 25.

1254
01:29:04,947 --> 01:29:06,490
Fuck this place!

1255
01:29:06,491 --> 01:29:07,782
Fuck it!

1256
01:29:26,594 --> 01:29:29,346
I feel bad. I let him down.

1257
01:29:29,430 --> 01:29:31,515
Ah, that's crap, kid.

1258
01:29:31,516 --> 01:29:33,058
He's proud of you.

1259
01:29:33,142 --> 01:29:36,269
We've been friends a long time
so I know him as good as anybody.

1260
01:29:36,354 --> 01:29:39,231
- A smart fellow, ain't he?
- As smart as they come.

1261
01:29:39,315 --> 01:29:41,358
He used to be a banker on the outside.

1262
01:29:41,442 --> 01:29:43,485
What's he in here for, anyway?

1263
01:29:43,569 --> 01:29:45,070
Murder.

1264
01:29:46,197 --> 01:29:47,948
The hell you say!

1265
01:29:50,076 --> 01:29:52,369
You wouldn't think it to look at the guy.

1266
01:29:53,412 --> 01:29:55,872
He caught his wife in bed with some golf pro.

1267
01:29:55,957 --> 01:29:58,041
He greased them both.

1268
01:30:05,925 --> 01:30:07,801
What?

1269
01:30:07,885 --> 01:30:10,262
About four years ago,

1270
01:30:10,346 --> 01:30:13,431
I was in Thomaston on a two-to-three stretch.

1271
01:30:14,684 --> 01:30:17,769
I stole a car. It was a dumb-fuck thing to do.

1272
01:30:19,063 --> 01:30:21,106
About six months left to go,

1273
01:30:21,190 --> 01:30:23,233
I get a new cellmate in.

1274
01:30:23,317 --> 01:30:25,193
Elmo Blatch.

1275
01:30:26,237 --> 01:30:27,821
Big, twitchy fucker.

1276
01:30:27,822 --> 01:30:30,115
Kind of roomie you pray you don't get.

1277
01:30:30,199 --> 01:30:32,242
You know what I'm saying?

1278
01:30:32,326 --> 01:30:34,369
Six-to-twelve, armed burglary.

1279
01:30:34,453 --> 01:30:37,664
He said he'd pulled hundreds of jobs.

1280
01:30:37,748 --> 01:30:39,624
Hard to believe, high-strung as he was.

1281
01:30:39,709 --> 01:30:42,711
You'd cut a loud fart,
he'd jump three feet in the air.

1282
01:30:43,838 --> 01:30:45,880
Talked all the time, too.

1283
01:30:45,965 --> 01:30:48,300
That's the other thing. He never shut up.

1284
01:30:48,384 --> 01:30:50,427
Places he'd been in,

1285
01:30:50,511 --> 01:30:53,179
jobs he'd pulled, women he'd fucked.

1286
01:30:53,264 --> 01:30:55,682
Even people he'd killed.

1287
01:30:57,518 --> 01:30:59,894
People who gave him shit.

1288
01:31:00,938 --> 01:31:02,981
That's how he put it.

1289
01:31:03,065 --> 01:31:04,608
So...

1290
01:31:04,692 --> 01:31:06,776
one night, like a joke,

1291
01:31:07,820 --> 01:31:11,114
I say to him, I say,
"Yeah, Elmo, who did you kill?"

1292
01:31:11,198 --> 01:31:12,991
So he says...

1293
01:31:13,075 --> 01:31:16,703
I got me this job one time
bussing tables at a country club.

1294
01:31:17,830 --> 01:31:21,416
So I could case
all these big rich pricks that come in.

1295
01:31:23,836 --> 01:31:25,837
So I pick out this guy,

1296
01:31:26,881 --> 01:31:29,466
go in one night and do his place.

1297
01:31:31,636 --> 01:31:33,637
He wakes up.

1298
01:31:33,721 --> 01:31:35,764
He gives me shit.

1299
01:31:36,807 --> 01:31:38,892
So I killed him.

1300
01:31:39,935 --> 01:31:42,020
Him and this tasty bitch he was with.

1301
01:31:44,315 --> 01:31:46,232
That's the best part.

1302
01:31:47,276 --> 01:31:50,487
She's fucking this prick, see, this golf pro.

1303
01:31:50,571 --> 01:31:52,656
But she's married to some other guy.

1304
01:31:53,699 --> 01:31:55,784
Some hotshot banker.

1305
01:31:57,578 --> 01:31:59,663
And he's the one they pinned it on.

1306
01:32:10,800 --> 01:32:14,636
I have to say
that's the most amazing story I ever heard.

1307
01:32:16,263 --> 01:32:19,140
What amazes me most is
you were taken in by it.

1308
01:32:19,225 --> 01:32:20,850
Sir?

1309
01:32:20,935 --> 01:32:22,602
Well...

1310
01:32:22,687 --> 01:32:26,064
it's obvious this fella Williams
is impressed with you.

1311
01:32:26,148 --> 01:32:28,233
He hears your tale of woe

1312
01:32:28,317 --> 01:32:31,236
and quite naturally wants to cheer you up.

1313
01:32:31,320 --> 01:32:33,363
He's young, not terribly bright.

1314
01:32:33,447 --> 01:32:37,325
Not surprising he wouldn't know
what a state he'd put you in.

1315
01:32:37,410 --> 01:32:39,536
Sir, he's telling the truth.

1316
01:32:39,620 --> 01:32:43,665
Well, let's say, for the moment,
this Blatch does exist.

1317
01:32:43,749 --> 01:32:46,876
You think he'd just fall to his knees and cry,

1318
01:32:46,961 --> 01:32:49,170
"Yes, I did it! I confess!

1319
01:32:49,255 --> 01:32:51,464
And by the way,
add a life term to my sentence"?

1320
01:32:51,549 --> 01:32:54,718
That doesn't matter.
With Tommy's testimony, I can get a new trial.

1321
01:32:54,802 --> 01:32:58,012
That's assuming Blatch is even still there.

1322
01:32:58,097 --> 01:33:00,140
The chances are he'd be released by now.

1323
01:33:00,224 --> 01:33:02,809
They'd have his last address,
names of relatives.

1324
01:33:02,893 --> 01:33:05,061
There's a chance, isn't there?

1325
01:33:07,106 --> 01:33:08,982
How can you be so obtuse?

1326
01:33:09,066 --> 01:33:10,567
What?

1327
01:33:12,069 --> 01:33:13,903
What did you call me?

1328
01:33:13,988 --> 01:33:15,822
Obtuse. Is it deliberate?

1329
01:33:15,906 --> 01:33:17,699
Son, you're forgetting yourself.

1330
01:33:17,783 --> 01:33:20,618
The country club will have his old timecards,

1331
01:33:20,703 --> 01:33:22,829
records, W-2s,

1332
01:33:22,913 --> 01:33:26,916
- with his name on them.
- If you indulge this fantasy, that's your business.

1333
01:33:27,001 --> 01:33:30,962
- Don't make it mine. This meeting is over.
- Sir, if I were to ever get out,

1334
01:33:31,046 --> 01:33:36,050
I would never mention what goes on in here.
I'd be as indictable as you for laundering money.

1335
01:33:37,845 --> 01:33:40,847
Don't ever mention money to me again,
you sorry son of a bitch!

1336
01:33:40,931 --> 01:33:42,807
Not in this office.

1337
01:33:42,808 --> 01:33:44,934
Not anywhere! Get in here now.

1338
01:33:45,019 --> 01:33:48,271
I'm just trying to set your mind at ease,
that's all. Sir, I...

1339
01:33:48,355 --> 01:33:49,731
Solitary. A month.

1340
01:33:49,815 --> 01:33:51,983
- Yes, sir.
- What's the matter with you?

1341
01:33:52,067 --> 01:33:55,320
- Get him out of here.
- This is my chance to get out! Don't you see?

1342
01:33:55,321 --> 01:33:57,322
It's my life! Don't you understand?

1343
01:33:57,406 --> 01:33:59,866
- Get him out! Get him out!
- This is my life!

1344
01:33:59,950 --> 01:34:01,201
No!

1345
01:34:01,285 --> 01:34:03,119
A month in the hole.

1346
01:34:03,204 --> 01:34:05,789
It's the longest damn stretch I ever heard of.

1347
01:34:05,873 --> 01:34:07,665
It's all my fault.

1348
01:34:07,750 --> 01:34:09,167
Oh, bullshit.

1349
01:34:09,251 --> 01:34:12,545
You didn't pull the trigger
and you certainly didn't convict him.

1350
01:34:12,630 --> 01:34:15,006
Red, are you saying that Andy is innocent?

1351
01:34:15,090 --> 01:34:17,258
I mean, for real innocent?

1352
01:34:17,343 --> 01:34:19,385
It looks that way.

1353
01:34:19,470 --> 01:34:21,513
Sweet Jesus.

1354
01:34:21,597 --> 01:34:23,640
How long's he been here now?

1355
01:34:23,724 --> 01:34:25,767
1947. What is that?

1356
01:34:25,851 --> 01:34:27,393
- 19 years.
TOMMY: 19 years...

1357
01:34:27,478 --> 01:34:30,104
GUARD: Williams, Thomas.
- Yeah, over here.

1358
01:34:32,483 --> 01:34:34,734
- Paxon, Edward.
- Yeah.

1359
01:34:35,945 --> 01:34:38,863
What you got? Board of Education.

1360
01:34:38,948 --> 01:34:41,616
- The son of a bitch mailed it.
- Looks like he did.

1361
01:34:41,700 --> 01:34:44,744
Are you gonna open it or stand there
with your thumb up your butt?

1362
01:34:44,829 --> 01:34:46,913
The thumb up my butt sounds better.

1363
01:34:47,957 --> 01:34:50,708
Hey, Skeet, come on,
give me that, you shithead.

1364
01:34:51,752 --> 01:34:53,962
- Floyd, come on.
- Over here, over here!

1365
01:34:55,005 --> 01:34:57,090
Hey, Red!

1366
01:34:58,217 --> 01:35:00,635
Come on, will you throw that away, please?

1367
01:35:06,225 --> 01:35:08,309
Well, shit.

1368
01:35:20,531 --> 01:35:23,241
The kid passed. C+ average.

1369
01:35:23,325 --> 01:35:25,410
Thought you'd like to know.

1370
01:35:41,969 --> 01:35:44,053
The Warden wants to talk.

1371
01:35:57,443 --> 01:35:58,568
Out here?

1372
01:35:58,652 --> 01:36:00,737
That's what the man said.

1373
01:36:17,046 --> 01:36:18,838
Warden?

1374
01:36:18,923 --> 01:36:21,007
Tommy.

1375
01:36:26,472 --> 01:36:27,931
Tommy...

1376
01:36:28,015 --> 01:36:32,101
I'm asking you to keep this conversation
just between us.

1377
01:36:33,520 --> 01:36:35,605
I feel awkward enough as it is.

1378
01:36:54,083 --> 01:36:56,167
We got a situation here.

1379
01:36:57,211 --> 01:36:59,253
I think you can appreciate that.

1380
01:36:59,338 --> 01:37:00,797
Yes, sir.

1381
01:37:00,881 --> 01:37:02,382
I sure can.

1382
01:37:02,466 --> 01:37:06,511
I tell you, son, this thing really came along
and knocked my wind out.

1383
01:37:08,222 --> 01:37:10,765
It's got me up nights, that's the truth.

1384
01:37:12,977 --> 01:37:15,061
The right thing to do.

1385
01:37:16,397 --> 01:37:19,399
Sometimes it's hard to know what that is.

1386
01:37:21,110 --> 01:37:23,152
You understand?

1387
01:37:24,530 --> 01:37:26,614
I need your help, son.

1388
01:37:28,033 --> 01:37:30,118
If I'm going to move on this,

1389
01:37:30,119 --> 01:37:32,870
there can't be the least little shred of doubt.

1390
01:37:34,415 --> 01:37:37,834
I have to know
if what you told Dufresne was the truth.

1391
01:37:37,918 --> 01:37:39,502
Yes, sir.

1392
01:37:39,503 --> 01:37:41,129
Absolutely.

1393
01:37:41,213 --> 01:37:44,298
Would you be willing
to swear before a judge and jury,

1394
01:37:44,383 --> 01:37:46,801
having placed your hand on the Good Book

1395
01:37:46,802 --> 01:37:49,512
and taken an oath before Almighty God himself?

1396
01:37:49,596 --> 01:37:51,723
Just give me that chance.

1397
01:37:55,644 --> 01:37:57,311
That's what I thought.

1398
01:38:44,401 --> 01:38:46,486
I'm sure by now you've heard...

1399
01:38:47,738 --> 01:38:49,113
A terrible thing.

1400
01:38:50,574 --> 01:38:54,160
A man that young, less than a year to go,
trying to escape.

1401
01:38:55,245 --> 01:38:57,872
It broke Captain Hadley's heart to shoot him.

1402
01:38:58,916 --> 01:39:01,000
Truly, it did.

1403
01:39:03,045 --> 01:39:05,129
We just have to put it behind us.

1404
01:39:07,299 --> 01:39:08,716
Move on.

1405
01:39:10,177 --> 01:39:12,053
I'm done.

1406
01:39:13,097 --> 01:39:15,181
Everything stops.

1407
01:39:16,225 --> 01:39:19,060
Get someone else to run your scams.

1408
01:39:20,395 --> 01:39:22,480
Nothing stops.

1409
01:39:23,524 --> 01:39:25,608
Nothing.

1410
01:39:27,361 --> 01:39:29,445
Or you will do the hardest time there is.

1411
01:39:31,323 --> 01:39:33,908
No more protection from the guards.

1412
01:39:33,992 --> 01:39:36,327
I'll pull you out of that one-bunk Hilton

1413
01:39:36,411 --> 01:39:38,579
and cast you down with the Sodomites.

1414
01:39:40,332 --> 01:39:42,834
You'll think you've been fucked by a train.

1415
01:39:44,128 --> 01:39:46,170
And the library?

1416
01:39:46,255 --> 01:39:47,380
Gone.

1417
01:39:47,464 --> 01:39:49,841
Sealed off, brick by brick.

1418
01:39:50,884 --> 01:39:53,678
We'll have us a little book barbecue in the yard.

1419
01:39:53,762 --> 01:39:55,805
They'll see the flames for miles.

1420
01:39:55,889 --> 01:39:58,391
We'll dance around it like wild Injuns.

1421
01:39:59,977 --> 01:40:01,894
You understand me?

1422
01:40:01,979 --> 01:40:04,063
Catching my drift?

1423
01:40:07,151 --> 01:40:09,235
Or am I being obtuse?

1424
01:40:20,372 --> 01:40:22,498
Give him another month to think about it.

1425
01:41:10,088 --> 01:41:13,132
My wife used to say I'm a hard man to know.

1426
01:41:13,217 --> 01:41:15,301
Like a closed book.

1427
01:41:16,511 --> 01:41:19,263
Complained about it all the time.

1428
01:41:19,348 --> 01:41:21,432
She was beautiful.

1429
01:41:23,644 --> 01:41:25,728
God, I loved her.

1430
01:41:28,732 --> 01:41:31,067
I just didn't know how to show it, that's all.

1431
01:41:33,570 --> 01:41:35,655
I killed her, Red.

1432
01:41:37,115 --> 01:41:39,200
I didn't pull the trigger.

1433
01:41:40,619 --> 01:41:42,703
But I drove her away.

1434
01:41:43,914 --> 01:41:46,457
That's why she died, because of me.

1435
01:41:46,541 --> 01:41:48,626
The way I am.

1436
01:41:56,969 --> 01:41:59,053
That don't make you a murderer.

1437
01:42:02,266 --> 01:42:04,350
A bad husband, maybe.

1438
01:42:07,729 --> 01:42:11,107
Feel bad about it if you want to,
but you didn't pull the trigger.

1439
01:42:11,191 --> 01:42:12,608
No, I didn't.

1440
01:42:12,693 --> 01:42:14,735
Somebody else did.

1441
01:42:16,029 --> 01:42:18,114
And I wound up in here.

1442
01:42:20,492 --> 01:42:22,576
Bad luck, I guess.

1443
01:42:25,497 --> 01:42:27,206
Yeah.

1444
01:42:27,291 --> 01:42:29,583
It floats around.

1445
01:42:29,668 --> 01:42:32,128
It's got to land on somebody.

1446
01:42:32,212 --> 01:42:34,463
It was my turn, that's all.

1447
01:42:35,549 --> 01:42:38,009
I was in the path of the tornado.

1448
01:42:44,599 --> 01:42:48,352
I just didn't expect
the storm would last as long as it has.

1449
01:42:52,441 --> 01:42:54,358
Do you think you'll ever get out of here?

1450
01:42:55,610 --> 01:42:57,278
Me?

1451
01:42:59,573 --> 01:43:01,032
Yeah.

1452
01:43:02,075 --> 01:43:04,035
One day, when I've got a long, white beard

1453
01:43:04,119 --> 01:43:07,621
and two or three marbles rolling around upstairs,
they'll let me out.

1454
01:43:09,541 --> 01:43:11,625
I tell you where I'd go.

1455
01:43:12,836 --> 01:43:14,462
Zihuatanejo.

1456
01:43:14,546 --> 01:43:16,464
Zi...what?

1457
01:43:16,548 --> 01:43:18,591
Zihuatanejo.

1458
01:43:18,675 --> 01:43:20,760
It's in Mexico.

1459
01:43:22,554 --> 01:43:24,764
A little place on the Pacific Ocean.

1460
01:43:26,016 --> 01:43:28,392
You know what the Mexicans say
about the Pacific?

1461
01:43:28,477 --> 01:43:29,935
No.

1462
01:43:30,020 --> 01:43:32,104
They say it has no memory.

1463
01:43:34,566 --> 01:43:37,109
That's where I want to live the rest of my life.

1464
01:43:38,695 --> 01:43:40,780
A warm place with no memory.

1465
01:43:44,076 --> 01:43:46,369
Open up a little hotel,

1466
01:43:48,205 --> 01:43:50,081
right on the beach.

1467
01:43:50,165 --> 01:43:52,208
Buy some worthless old boat,

1468
01:43:52,292 --> 01:43:54,377
fix it up new.

1469
01:43:57,339 --> 01:43:58,547
Take my guests out.

1470
01:43:58,632 --> 01:44:00,716
Charter fishing.

1471
01:44:04,638 --> 01:44:07,264
Zihuatanejo, huh?

1472
01:44:09,893 --> 01:44:13,771
In a place like that, I could use a man
who knows how to get things.

1473
01:44:20,195 --> 01:44:22,905
I don't think I could make it on the outside, Andy.

1474
01:44:29,663 --> 01:44:31,747
I've been in here most of my life.

1475
01:44:33,250 --> 01:44:35,042
I'm an institutional man now.

1476
01:44:35,127 --> 01:44:37,211
Just like Brooks was.

1477
01:44:38,630 --> 01:44:40,881
Well, you underestimate yourself.

1478
01:44:40,966 --> 01:44:43,050
I don't think so.

1479
01:44:45,679 --> 01:44:49,890
I mean...in here, I'm the guy
who can get things for you, sure.

1480
01:44:49,975 --> 01:44:53,894
But outside, all you need is the Yellow Pages.

1481
01:44:53,979 --> 01:44:56,397
Hell, I wouldn't even know where to begin.

1482
01:44:58,483 --> 01:45:00,818
The Pacific Ocean?

1483
01:45:00,902 --> 01:45:02,945
Shit.

1484
01:45:03,029 --> 01:45:06,198
- About scare me to death, something that big.
- Not me.

1485
01:45:06,283 --> 01:45:09,743
I didn't shoot my wife and I didn't shoot her lover.

1486
01:45:11,663 --> 01:45:15,291
Whatever mistakes I've made,
I've paid for them and then some.

1487
01:45:15,375 --> 01:45:17,543
That hotel, that boat.

1488
01:45:19,129 --> 01:45:21,297
I don't think that's too much to ask.

1489
01:45:25,927 --> 01:45:28,804
I don't think you ought to be
doing this to yourself, Andy.

1490
01:45:28,889 --> 01:45:30,931
These are just shitty pipe dreams.

1491
01:45:31,016 --> 01:45:33,976
I mean, Mexico is way the hell down there
and you're in here.

1492
01:45:34,060 --> 01:45:36,103
And that's the way it is.

1493
01:45:36,188 --> 01:45:38,814
Yeah, right. That's the way it is.

1494
01:45:40,567 --> 01:45:42,651
It's down there and I'm in here.

1495
01:45:45,363 --> 01:45:48,157
I guess it comes down to a simple choice, really.

1496
01:45:50,994 --> 01:45:53,037
Get busy living

1497
01:45:53,121 --> 01:45:56,040
or get busy dying.

1498
01:45:59,836 --> 01:46:01,921
Andy.

1499
01:46:04,382 --> 01:46:06,467
Red.

1500
01:46:07,511 --> 01:46:09,929
If you ever get out of here, do me a favor.

1501
01:46:10,013 --> 01:46:11,847
Sure, Andy.

1502
01:46:11,932 --> 01:46:13,265
Anything.

1503
01:46:14,351 --> 01:46:17,811
There's a big hayfield up near Buxton.
You know where Buxton is?

1504
01:46:19,814 --> 01:46:23,609
- Well, just... There's a lot of hayfields up there.
- One in particular.

1505
01:46:23,693 --> 01:46:25,736
It's got a long rock wall

1506
01:46:25,820 --> 01:46:27,738
with a big oak tree at the north end.

1507
01:46:27,822 --> 01:46:30,533
It's like something out of a Robert Frost poem.

1508
01:46:31,910 --> 01:46:34,620
It's where I asked my wife to marry me.

1509
01:46:35,830 --> 01:46:39,625
We went there for a picnic
and made love under that oak.

1510
01:46:41,127 --> 01:46:43,212
I asked and she said yes.

1511
01:46:47,342 --> 01:46:49,385
Promise me, Red,

1512
01:46:49,469 --> 01:46:51,470
if you ever get out,

1513
01:46:51,555 --> 01:46:53,597
find that spot.

1514
01:46:54,641 --> 01:47:00,104
At the base of that wall, you'll find a rock that has
no earthly business in a Maine hayfield.

1515
01:47:00,188 --> 01:47:02,690
A piece of black, volcanic glass.

1516
01:47:05,860 --> 01:47:08,654
There's something buried under it
I want you to have.

1517
01:47:08,738 --> 01:47:10,739
What, Andy?

1518
01:47:10,824 --> 01:47:12,908
What's buried under there?

1519
01:47:14,703 --> 01:47:18,080
You'll have to pry it up to see.

1520
01:47:23,420 --> 01:47:25,504
No, I'm telling you, the guy is...

1521
01:47:26,548 --> 01:47:28,632
He's talking funnier.

1522
01:47:28,717 --> 01:47:32,094
- I'm really worried about him.
- We ought to keep an eye on him.

1523
01:47:32,178 --> 01:47:36,223
That's fine during the day,
but at night he's got that cell all to himself.

1524
01:47:37,309 --> 01:47:38,767
Oh, Lord.

1525
01:47:38,852 --> 01:47:40,894
What?

1526
01:47:42,564 --> 01:47:44,898
Andy come down to the loading dock today.

1527
01:47:44,983 --> 01:47:48,485
- He asked me for a length of rope.
- Rope?

1528
01:47:48,570 --> 01:47:50,404
Six feet long.

1529
01:47:50,488 --> 01:47:51,947
And you gave it to him?

1530
01:47:52,032 --> 01:47:54,700
Sure I did. Why wouldn't I?

1531
01:47:54,701 --> 01:47:56,410
Jesus, Heywood.

1532
01:47:56,494 --> 01:47:58,746
Hey, how the hell was I supposed to know?

1533
01:47:58,830 --> 01:48:00,748
Remember Brooks Hatlen?

1534
01:48:00,832 --> 01:48:01,874
No.

1535
01:48:02,917 --> 01:48:05,127
Andy would never do that.

1536
01:48:05,128 --> 01:48:07,212
Never.

1537
01:48:08,256 --> 01:48:10,341
I don't know.

1538
01:48:12,385 --> 01:48:14,470
Every man has his breaking point.

1539
01:48:17,098 --> 01:48:18,349
Lickety-split.

1540
01:48:18,433 --> 01:48:21,310
- I want to get home.
- Just about finished, sir.

1541
01:48:37,410 --> 01:48:39,495
Three deposits tonight.

1542
01:48:47,879 --> 01:48:49,963
Get my stuff down to the laundry.

1543
01:48:49,964 --> 01:48:53,425
And shine my shoes.
I want 'em looking like mirrors.

1544
01:48:53,510 --> 01:48:54,885
Yes, sir.

1545
01:49:00,183 --> 01:49:01,850
It's good having you back, Andy.

1546
01:49:01,935 --> 01:49:04,687
The place wasn't the same without you.

1547
01:49:51,735 --> 01:49:54,486
GUARD: Lights out!

1548
01:50:25,226 --> 01:50:27,519
RED: I've had some long nights in stir.

1549
01:50:28,563 --> 01:50:31,273
Alone in the dark with nothing but your thoughts,

1550
01:50:31,357 --> 01:50:33,192
time can draw out like a blade.

1551
01:50:39,574 --> 01:50:41,909
That was the longest night of my life.

1552
01:50:58,051 --> 01:50:59,676
GUARD: Give me a count.

1553
01:51:04,015 --> 01:51:06,058
Tier 3 South. Clear.

1554
01:51:06,142 --> 01:51:07,476
Tier 4 North. Clear.

1555
01:51:07,560 --> 01:51:11,146
- Tier 3 North. Clear.
- Man missing on Tier 2.

1556
01:51:11,231 --> 01:51:14,316
- Cell 245.
- Dufresne.

1557
01:51:14,400 --> 01:51:17,319
Get your ass out here, boy.
You're holding up the show.

1558
01:51:19,823 --> 01:51:23,867
Don't make me come down there.
I'll thump your skull for you.

1559
01:51:31,459 --> 01:51:34,837
Damn it, Dufresne, you're putting me behind!
I got a schedule to keep.

1560
01:51:34,921 --> 01:51:38,006
You'd better be sick or dead in there,
I shit you not!

1561
01:51:38,091 --> 01:51:39,925
You hear me?

1562
01:51:46,432 --> 01:51:48,517
Oh, my holy God.

1563
01:52:05,368 --> 01:52:08,078
I want every man on this cell block questioned.

1564
01:52:08,079 --> 01:52:10,289
- Start with that friend of his.
- Who?

1565
01:52:10,373 --> 01:52:11,582
Him!

1566
01:52:13,501 --> 01:52:15,586
Open 237.

1567
01:52:18,590 --> 01:52:20,716
What do you mean, he just wasn't here?

1568
01:52:20,800 --> 01:52:22,342
Don't say that to me, Haig.

1569
01:52:22,427 --> 01:52:24,678
Don't say that to me again.

1570
01:52:24,762 --> 01:52:25,846
But, sir, he wasn't.

1571
01:52:25,930 --> 01:52:28,098
I can see that, Haig!

1572
01:52:28,182 --> 01:52:29,975
You think I'm blind?

1573
01:52:29,976 --> 01:52:33,103
Is that what you're saying? Am I blind, Haig?

1574
01:52:33,187 --> 01:52:34,187
No, sir.

1575
01:52:34,272 --> 01:52:36,315
What about you?

1576
01:52:36,399 --> 01:52:38,233
Are you blind?

1577
01:52:38,318 --> 01:52:40,861
- Tell me what this is.
- Last night's count.

1578
01:52:40,945 --> 01:52:44,239
You see Dufresne's name there?
I sure do. See? Right there.

1579
01:52:44,324 --> 01:52:46,199
Dufresne.

1580
01:52:47,452 --> 01:52:49,119
He was in his cell at lights out.

1581
01:52:49,203 --> 01:52:52,414
It stands to reason
he'd still be here in the morning.

1582
01:52:53,499 --> 01:52:55,375
I want him found.

1583
01:52:55,460 --> 01:52:58,045
Not tomorrow, not after breakfast.

1584
01:52:58,129 --> 01:52:59,171
Now!

1585
01:52:59,172 --> 01:53:00,672
Yes, sir!

1586
01:53:01,799 --> 01:53:04,384
Let's go, let's go!
Move your butts! Move it!

1587
01:53:07,221 --> 01:53:08,263
Stand.

1588
01:53:09,390 --> 01:53:10,641
Well?

1589
01:53:14,020 --> 01:53:15,145
Well, what?

1590
01:53:15,229 --> 01:53:18,690
I see you two all the time.
You're thick as thieves, you are.

1591
01:53:19,734 --> 01:53:21,360
He must have said something.

1592
01:53:21,444 --> 01:53:23,487
No, sir, Warden.

1593
01:53:23,571 --> 01:53:25,030
Not a word.

1594
01:53:27,033 --> 01:53:29,034
Lord, it's a miracle!

1595
01:53:30,078 --> 01:53:32,537
A man up and vanished like a fart in the wind.

1596
01:53:32,622 --> 01:53:34,373
Nothing left but...

1597
01:53:34,457 --> 01:53:37,376
some damn rocks on a windowsill.

1598
01:53:37,460 --> 01:53:39,211
And that cupcake on the wall.

1599
01:53:39,295 --> 01:53:40,921
Let's ask her.

1600
01:53:41,005 --> 01:53:44,967
Maybe she knows. What say there,
Fuzzy Britches? Feel like talking?

1601
01:53:46,052 --> 01:53:47,135
Ah...

1602
01:53:47,136 --> 01:53:48,595
I guess not.

1603
01:53:48,680 --> 01:53:51,556
Why should she be any different?

1604
01:53:51,641 --> 01:53:54,101
This is a conspiracy.

1605
01:53:54,185 --> 01:53:56,395
That's what this is.

1606
01:53:56,479 --> 01:53:58,021
One big...

1607
01:53:58,106 --> 01:54:00,857
damn conspiracy!

1608
01:54:00,942 --> 01:54:03,610
And everyone's in on it!

1609
01:54:03,695 --> 01:54:04,987
Including her!

1610
01:54:39,647 --> 01:54:41,523
RED: In 1966,

1611
01:54:41,607 --> 01:54:44,276
Andy Dufresne escaped

1612
01:54:44,360 --> 01:54:46,445
from Shawshank Prison.

1613
01:54:48,114 --> 01:54:50,866
All they found of him
was a muddy set of prison clothes,

1614
01:54:50,950 --> 01:54:52,993
a bar of soap

1615
01:54:53,077 --> 01:54:57,456
and an old rock hammer
damn near worn down to the nub.

1616
01:55:01,836 --> 01:55:06,673
I remember thinking it would take a man
600 years to tunnel through the wall with it.

1617
01:55:06,758 --> 01:55:09,468
Old Andy did it in less than 20.

1618
01:55:28,029 --> 01:55:30,113
Oh, Andy loved geology.

1619
01:55:31,157 --> 01:55:34,659
I imagine it appealed to his meticulous nature.

1620
01:55:34,744 --> 01:55:37,037
An Ice Age here,

1621
01:55:37,121 --> 01:55:39,706
a million years of mountain-building there.

1622
01:55:40,750 --> 01:55:43,168
Geology is the study of pressure and time.

1623
01:55:44,378 --> 01:55:46,421
That's all it takes, really.

1624
01:55:46,506 --> 01:55:48,548
Pressure...

1625
01:55:48,633 --> 01:55:50,509
and time.

1626
01:55:50,593 --> 01:55:53,929
That and a big goddamn poster.

1627
01:55:57,141 --> 01:55:58,517
Like I said,

1628
01:55:58,518 --> 01:56:02,312
in prison, a man will do most anything
to keep his mind occupied.

1629
01:56:05,108 --> 01:56:10,278
It turns out Andy's favorite hobby
was toting his wall out into the exercise yard.

1630
01:56:10,363 --> 01:56:12,447
A handful at a time.

1631
01:56:14,283 --> 01:56:16,451
I guess after Tommy was killed,

1632
01:56:16,536 --> 01:56:19,371
Andy decided he'd been here
just about long enough.

1633
01:56:19,455 --> 01:56:21,665
Lickety-split. I want to get home.

1634
01:56:22,959 --> 01:56:25,043
I'm just about finished, sir.

1635
01:56:46,440 --> 01:56:48,525
Three deposits tonight.

1636
01:56:55,700 --> 01:56:57,742
Andy did like he was told.

1637
01:56:58,786 --> 01:57:02,289
Buffed those shoes to a high mirror shine.

1638
01:57:05,877 --> 01:57:07,919
The guard simply didn't notice.

1639
01:57:08,004 --> 01:57:10,046
Neither did I.

1640
01:57:10,131 --> 01:57:14,467
I mean, seriously, how often
do you really look at a man's shoes?

1641
01:59:18,509 --> 01:59:24,681
Andy crawled to freedom through 500 yards
of shit-smelling foulness I can't even imagine.

1642
01:59:26,017 --> 01:59:28,018
Or maybe I just don't want to.

1643
01:59:32,732 --> 01:59:34,941
500 yards.

1644
01:59:35,985 --> 01:59:38,486
That's the length of five football fields.

1645
01:59:39,530 --> 01:59:41,781
Just shy of half a mile.

1646
02:00:37,004 --> 02:00:41,549
The next morning, right about the time
Raquel was spilling her little secret,

1647
02:00:41,634 --> 02:00:45,053
a man nobody ever laid eyes on before

1648
02:00:45,137 --> 02:00:47,555
strode into the Maine National Bank.

1649
02:00:47,640 --> 02:00:49,682
Until that moment,

1650
02:00:49,767 --> 02:00:51,643
he didn't exist,

1651
02:00:51,727 --> 02:00:53,061
except on paper.

1652
02:00:53,145 --> 02:00:54,187
May I help you?

1653
02:00:54,271 --> 02:00:56,314
RED: He had all the proper ID,

1654
02:00:56,399 --> 02:01:00,026
driver's license, birth certificate,
social security card.

1655
02:01:00,111 --> 02:01:03,405
And the signature was a spot-on match.

1656
02:01:03,489 --> 02:01:06,533
I must say, I'm sorry to be losing your business.

1657
02:01:06,617 --> 02:01:08,618
I hope you'll enjoy living abroad.

1658
02:01:08,702 --> 02:01:10,495
Thank you.

1659
02:01:10,579 --> 02:01:12,372
I'm sure I will.

1660
02:01:12,456 --> 02:01:14,582
Here's your cashier's check, sir.

1661
02:01:14,667 --> 02:01:16,709
Will there be anything else?

1662
02:01:16,794 --> 02:01:17,794
Please...

1663
02:01:17,878 --> 02:01:20,964
Would you add this to your outgoing mail?

1664
02:01:21,048 --> 02:01:23,258
I'd be happy to.

1665
02:01:23,342 --> 02:01:25,677
- Good day, sir.
- Good day.

1666
02:01:25,761 --> 02:01:30,557
RED: Mr. Stevens visited nearly a dozen banks
in the Portland area that morning.

1667
02:01:31,725 --> 02:01:38,189
All told, he blew town with better than $370,000
of Warden Norton's money.

1668
02:01:38,274 --> 02:01:40,733
Severance pay for 19 years.

1669
02:01:48,868 --> 02:01:51,161
Good morning, Portland Daily Bugle.

1670
02:02:43,881 --> 02:02:46,883
Byron Hadley?
You have the right to remain silent.

1671
02:02:46,967 --> 02:02:51,596
If you give up the right to remain silent, anything
you say will be held against you in court.

1672
02:02:51,680 --> 02:02:53,556
RED: I wasn't there to see it.

1673
02:02:53,641 --> 02:02:58,520
But I hear Byron Hadley started sobbing
like a little girl when they took him away.

1674
02:03:03,943 --> 02:03:06,736
Norton had no intention of going that quietly.

1675
02:03:22,711 --> 02:03:24,170
Samuel Norton?

1676
02:03:24,255 --> 02:03:27,507
- We have a warrant for your arrest. Open up.

1677
02:03:30,386 --> 02:03:31,511
Norton!

1678
02:03:31,595 --> 02:03:33,263
Open the door.

1679
02:03:33,347 --> 02:03:35,098
I'm not sure which key it is.

1680
02:03:37,977 --> 02:03:39,644
Norton!

1681
02:03:45,109 --> 02:03:47,193
Make it easy on yourself, Norton.

1682
02:03:58,497 --> 02:04:02,083
RED: I like to think
the last thing that went through his head,

1683
02:04:02,167 --> 02:04:03,876
other than that bullet,

1684
02:04:03,961 --> 02:04:07,964
was to wonder how the hell
Andy Dufresne ever got the best of him.

1685
02:04:09,633 --> 02:04:10,717
Carter, James...

1686
02:04:10,801 --> 02:04:14,178
RED: Not long after the Warden
deprived us of his company,

1687
02:04:14,263 --> 02:04:16,514
I got a postcard in the mail.

1688
02:04:18,225 --> 02:04:20,643
It was blank but the postmark said...

1689
02:04:20,728 --> 02:04:23,479
Fort Hancock, Texas.

1690
02:04:23,564 --> 02:04:25,607
Fort Hancock.

1691
02:04:25,691 --> 02:04:27,734
Right on the border.

1692
02:04:27,818 --> 02:04:29,861
That's where Andy crossed.

1693
02:04:29,945 --> 02:04:33,823
When I picture him heading south
in his own car with the top down,

1694
02:04:34,867 --> 02:04:36,951
it always makes me laugh.

1695
02:04:38,370 --> 02:04:40,413
Andy Dufresne,

1696
02:04:40,497 --> 02:04:42,874
who crawled through a river of shit

1697
02:04:42,958 --> 02:04:45,043
and came out clean on the other side.

1698
02:04:46,253 --> 02:04:48,296
Andy Dufresne,

1699
02:04:48,380 --> 02:04:50,298
headed for the Pacific.

1700
02:04:52,885 --> 02:04:54,927
Hadley's got him by the toes, right?

1701
02:04:55,012 --> 02:04:59,223
And he says, "I believe this boy's
about to have himself an accident."

1702
02:04:59,308 --> 02:05:02,477
RED: Those of us who knew him best
talk about him often.

1703
02:05:02,561 --> 02:05:04,812
I swear, the stuff he pulled...

1704
02:05:04,897 --> 02:05:07,398
"These friends of mine
could use a couple of beers!"

1705
02:05:07,483 --> 02:05:09,567
- And he got it.
- And he got it!

1706
02:05:10,736 --> 02:05:13,112
RED: Sometimes it makes me sad, though,

1707
02:05:13,197 --> 02:05:15,239
Andy being gone.

1708
02:05:15,324 --> 02:05:19,327
I have to remind myself
that some birds aren't meant to be caged.

1709
02:05:20,412 --> 02:05:22,497
Their feathers are just too bright.

1710
02:05:24,041 --> 02:05:26,084
And when they fly away,

1711
02:05:26,168 --> 02:05:30,088
the part of you that knows
it was a sin to lock them up does rejoice.

1712
02:05:31,840 --> 02:05:33,299
But still...

1713
02:05:33,384 --> 02:05:37,553
the place you live in is that much more drab
and empty that they're gone.

1714
02:05:42,101 --> 02:05:44,352
I guess I just miss my friend.

1715
02:06:08,335 --> 02:06:10,211
Please sit down.

1716
02:06:15,801 --> 02:06:17,927
Ellis Boyd Redding.

1717
02:06:18,011 --> 02:06:21,389
Your files say you've served
40 years of a life sentence.

1718
02:06:21,473 --> 02:06:23,850
Do you feel you've been rehabilitated?

1719
02:06:25,978 --> 02:06:28,062
Rehabilitated?

1720
02:06:29,606 --> 02:06:31,691
Well, now, let me see.

1721
02:06:32,735 --> 02:06:35,069
You know, I don't have any idea
what that means.

1722
02:06:36,864 --> 02:06:39,407
Well, it means you're ready to rejoin society...

1723
02:06:39,491 --> 02:06:42,034
I know what you think it means, sonny.

1724
02:06:44,413 --> 02:06:46,497
To me, it's just a made-up word.

1725
02:06:47,541 --> 02:06:49,792
A politician's word so that...

1726
02:06:49,877 --> 02:06:55,381
young fellas like yourself
can wear a suit and a tie and have a job.

1727
02:06:57,801 --> 02:06:59,886
What do you really want to know?

1728
02:07:01,054 --> 02:07:02,972
Am I sorry for what I did?

1729
02:07:03,056 --> 02:07:04,766
Well, are you?

1730
02:07:06,393 --> 02:07:08,978
There's not a day goes by I don't feel regret.

1731
02:07:10,564 --> 02:07:13,691
Not because I'm in here
or because you think I should.

1732
02:07:17,488 --> 02:07:19,572
I look back on the way I was then.

1733
02:07:22,159 --> 02:07:24,202
A young...

1734
02:07:24,286 --> 02:07:27,371
stupid kid who committed that terrible crime.

1735
02:07:31,210 --> 02:07:33,294
I wanna talk to him.

1736
02:07:34,671 --> 02:07:36,839
I wanna try and talk some sense to him,

1737
02:07:37,883 --> 02:07:39,967
tell him the way things are.

1738
02:07:42,137 --> 02:07:44,222
But I can't.

1739
02:07:45,432 --> 02:07:47,517
That kid's long gone.

1740
02:07:48,811 --> 02:07:50,895
This old man is all that's left.

1741
02:07:53,023 --> 02:07:54,524
I gotta live with that.

1742
02:07:55,859 --> 02:07:57,652
Rehabilitated?

1743
02:07:57,736 --> 02:07:59,737
It's just a bullshit word.

1744
02:07:59,822 --> 02:08:05,743
So you go on and stamp your form, sonny,
and stop wasting my time.

1745
02:08:06,829 --> 02:08:08,871
Because to tell you the truth,

1746
02:08:08,956 --> 02:08:11,457
I don't give a shit.

1747
02:09:37,669 --> 02:09:39,754
Here you go, miss.

1748
02:09:41,757 --> 02:09:43,215
Rest room break, boss?

1749
02:09:49,264 --> 02:09:53,559
You don't need to ask me every time you need
to go take a piss. Just go. Understand?

1750
02:09:54,770 --> 02:09:56,437
Yes, sir.

1751
02:10:06,490 --> 02:10:09,533
RED: 40 years
I've been asking permission to piss.

1752
02:10:10,535 --> 02:10:12,745
I can't squeeze a drop without say-so.

1753
02:10:16,875 --> 02:10:18,960
There's a harsh truth to face.

1754
02:10:20,921 --> 02:10:23,255
No way I'm gonna make it on the outside.

1755
02:10:31,473 --> 02:10:34,934
All I do any more
is think of ways to break my parole...

1756
02:10:35,978 --> 02:10:38,062
..so maybe they'd send me back.

1757
02:10:43,568 --> 02:10:45,653
A terrible thing to live in fear.

1758
02:10:47,030 --> 02:10:50,408
Brooks Hatlen knew it, knew it all too well.

1759
02:10:52,577 --> 02:10:55,371
All I want is to be back
where things make sense,

1760
02:10:56,415 --> 02:10:58,916
where I won't have to be afraid all the time.

1761
02:11:01,795 --> 02:11:03,879
Only one thing stops me.

1762
02:11:04,923 --> 02:11:07,008
A promise I made to Andy.

1763
02:11:27,738 --> 02:11:29,822
Here it is.

1764
02:11:39,624 --> 02:11:41,709
Much obliged, sir.

1765
02:15:20,512 --> 02:15:21,804
ANDY: Dear Red,

1766
02:15:21,888 --> 02:15:24,348
if you're reading this, you've gotten out.

1767
02:15:24,432 --> 02:15:28,686
And if you've come this far,
maybe you're willing to come a little further.

1768
02:15:28,770 --> 02:15:31,355
You remember the name of the town, don't you?

1769
02:15:33,650 --> 02:15:35,734
Zihuatanejo.

1770
02:15:36,778 --> 02:15:37,987
I could use a good man

1771
02:15:38,071 --> 02:15:40,114
to help me get my project on wheels.

1772
02:15:40,198 --> 02:15:42,324
I'll keep an eye out for you

1773
02:15:42,409 --> 02:15:44,451
and the chessboard ready.

1774
02:15:45,495 --> 02:15:46,870
Remember, Red,

1775
02:15:46,955 --> 02:15:48,998
hope is a good thing.

1776
02:15:49,082 --> 02:15:51,125
Maybe the best of things.

1777
02:15:51,209 --> 02:15:53,294
And no good thing ever dies.

1778
02:15:54,337 --> 02:15:56,797
I will be hoping that this letter finds you

1779
02:15:56,881 --> 02:15:58,924
and finds you well.

1780
02:15:59,009 --> 02:16:01,510
Your friend, Andy.

1781
02:16:36,087 --> 02:16:39,965
RED: Get busy living or get busy dying.

1782
02:16:42,469 --> 02:16:44,553
That's goddamn right.

1783
02:16:50,060 --> 02:16:52,186
For the second time in my life,

1784
02:16:52,270 --> 02:16:54,355
I'm guilty of committing a crime.

1785
02:16:56,024 --> 02:16:58,108
Parole violation.

1786
02:16:58,109 --> 02:17:01,820
Of course, I doubt they'll toss up
any roadblocks for that.

1787
02:17:03,073 --> 02:17:05,115
Not for an old crook like me.

1788
02:17:05,200 --> 02:17:07,284
Fort Hancock, Texas, please.

1789
02:17:10,997 --> 02:17:15,125
I find I'm so excited I can barely sit still
or hold a thought in my head.

1790
02:17:16,169 --> 02:17:19,171
I think it's the excitement
only a free man can feel.

1791
02:17:19,255 --> 02:17:21,965
A free man at the start of a long journey

1792
02:17:22,050 --> 02:17:24,968
whose conclusion is uncertain.

1793
02:17:27,847 --> 02:17:30,015
I hope I can make it across the border.

1794
02:17:31,893 --> 02:17:34,686
I hope to see my friend and shake his hand.

1795
02:17:37,065 --> 02:17:40,275
I hope the Pacific is as blue
as it has been in my dreams.

1796
02:17:43,029 --> 02:17:44,905
I hope.
